__kbPart({"name":"Omnicor-AI","content":{"Omnicor-AI-Developer-Guide":"---\ntype: dev-guide\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, dev-guide]\n---\n# Omnicor AI — Developer Guide (hackathon edition)\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](Omnicor-AI.md) · [Product portfolio](Omnicor-AI-Products.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Omnicor-AI-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> **Read this first.** OmniCor AI publishes **no SDK, no docs, no repos, no specs**. There is nothing to install before you talk to them. This guide therefore covers (a) what is publicly known, (b) what to ask at their table, and (c) a vendor-neutral fallback so your team is not blocked if their tooling is not self-serve. Anything about OC Agent / OC Node internals is **not public**.\n\n## TL;DR — how you program it\n\n- **You do not program OmniCor directly (as far as is public).** The stated workflow is: you bring a **trained model + a target hardware platform**, they deliver a deployment that fits your **power, speed and memory** limits.\n- **It sits at the inference layer**, not the robot-control layer: below your policy / agent code, above the chip. Robot motion still goes through the robot vendor's SDK (AgiBot, Galbot, Unitree, ...).\n- **Two named building blocks:** **OC Agent** (agentic deployment environment, software) and **OC Node** (in-house compute platform, hardware). Interfaces, APIs and specs for both: **not public**.\n- **Models they say they deploy:** Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek (LLMs) and **OpenVLA, pi0.5** (robot VLAs). All are third-party open models you can already run yourself.\n- **Hackathon play:** prototype with the open model on a laptop GPU first, then ask OmniCor to move it **on-board / offline**. Decide in the first hour whether their path fits in 48 h; if not, keep your laptop as the inference server.\n\n## SDKs and interfaces\n\n**OmniCor's own interfaces**\n\n| SDK / repo | Language | What it controls | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OC Agent | not public | Automates fitting a model to a target device (per site: \"agentic deployment environment\") | [site](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech) | No download, CLI, API or docs published. Access model (hosted / on-prem / operated by their engineers) not public |\n| OC Node | not public | Compute box that runs the deployed model | [site](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech) | Chip, memory, power, I/O, OS: not public |\n| GitHub account `OmnicorAI` | — | — | [github.com/OmnicorAI](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) | Account exists, **0 public repositories**; not linked from the official site, ownership ⚠️ unverified |\n| Docs portal / Hugging Face org / PyPI package | — | — | — | None found (docs subdomain does not resolve; no HF org under obvious names) |\n\n**What you will actually code against (third-party, verified)**\n\n| SDK / repo | Language | What it controls | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LeRobot (`lerobot[pi]`) | Python | pi0.5 policy: fine-tune, eval, rollout on a robot | [docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05) | `policy.type=pi05`; checkpoints `lerobot/pi05_base`, `lerobot/pi05_libero_base`; LeRobot docs state Apache 2.0, but the `lerobot/pi05_base` weights card is tagged `gemma` licence — check before reuse |\n| openpi | Python | Physical Intelligence reference implementation of pi0 / pi0.5 | [repo](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi) | LeRobot's pi0.5 is adapted from it |\n| OpenVLA | Python (PyTorch, HF Transformers) | 7-DoF end-effector actions from one image + instruction | [repo](https://github.com/openvla/openvla) | `openvla-7b` checkpoint, about **7.5B** params per the repo; `vla-scripts/deploy.py` exposes a REST server; code MIT, weights inherit Llama-2 licence terms |\n| Apertus (swiss-ai) | any HF-compatible runtime | Swiss open LLM, multilingual | [HF org](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai) | Sizes on the Hub include `Apertus-v1.1-0.5B / 1.5B / 4B(-Instruct)`, `Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509`, 70B, and multimodal `Apertus-v1.5-8B` |\n\n## Control levels\n\nNot a robot-control product, so \"high-level vs joint-level\" does not apply. Think of it as three layers:\n\n| Layer | Who owns it | What you do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Task / agent logic (what to do, when) | **You** | Your Python / ROS 2 code |\n| Model inference (VLA action chunks, LLM tokens, perception) | **OmniCor's layer** | Hand over model + hardware; get back a runtime that meets latency / power / memory targets |\n| Robot actuation (joint, torque, base, gripper) | **Robot vendor SDK** | Use that vendor's dev guide; OmniCor does not replace it |\n\n- **Rates:** nothing published. As a rule of thumb, a VLA runs at a few Hz to tens of Hz and outputs **action chunks**; the robot's own controller interpolates at its native rate. OmniCor has not published any Hz / latency figure for any model or chip.\n- **EDU / developer editions:** none exist publicly. No licence tiers known.\n\n## ROS 2 and middleware\n\n- **ROS 2 support: not stated** ⚠️ unverified. No ROS 2 package, message definitions or bridge are published.\n- **Practical pattern (works regardless of what they give you):** wrap the model behind a small local service and keep ROS 2 on the robot side.\n  - Robot side: vendor ROS 2 driver / SDK publishes camera + joint state, accepts action targets.\n  - Inference side: one process that takes `image(s) + state + instruction` and returns an action chunk (HTTP, gRPC or ZeroMQ on the LAN). OpenVLA's `deploy.py` already follows this shape.\n- **Ask on site:** \"Do you hand us a ROS 2 node, a REST / gRPC endpoint, or a library we link against?\"\n\n## Simulation and digital twins\n\n- **None from OmniCor.** No URDF / MJCF / USD assets, no Isaac Lab / MuJoCo / Gazebo repos.\n- Use the **robot vendor's** simulation assets, and LeRobot's simulation benchmarks (e.g. LIBERO, which the pi0.5 docs use for the reference fine-tune) to sanity-check a policy before touching hardware.\n- **Why it still matters here:** an optimised (quantised / compiled) model can behave differently from the original. Re-run the same eval episodes before and after optimisation and compare success rate, not just speed.\n\n## AI stack\n\n| Item | Status | What is known |\n|---|---|---|\n| Own pretrained policies / foundation model | none public | OmniCor deploys other people's models |\n| LLMs listed on site | ✅ third-party, open | Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, \"and others\" |\n| VLAs listed on site | ✅ third-party, open | OpenVLA, pi0.5 (site logo is labelled pi0.5; LeRobot ships it as `pi05`) |\n| Optimisation methods (quantisation, compilation, distillation, runtime) | not public | Site says only \"model optimisation\" for power / speed / memory |\n| Datasets | none public | — |\n| Teleoperation / data collection | none | Use the robot vendor's teleop + LeRobot recording |\n| LeRobot support | indirect | pi0.5 is a LeRobot policy; whether OC Agent ingests a LeRobot checkpoint directly is ⚠️ unverified |\n\n**Facts worth knowing before you hand a model over**\n\n- **pi0.5 (LeRobot):** install `pip install 'lerobot[pi]'`; needs the **gated** `google/paligemma-3b-pt-224` tokenizer (accept licence on the Hub, `hf auth login`). Default action **chunk size 50**. The reference full fine-tune is sized for a **single 80 GB GPU**; `train_expert_only=true` freezes the VLM and cuts memory. Supports Real-Time Chunking for latency-tolerant rollout.\n- **OpenVLA:** fused DINOv2 + SigLIP vision, Llama-2 language backbone, trained on **970K** Open X-Embodiment trajectories. Output is a single 7-DoF action un-normalised per dataset key (`unnorm_key`), so it needs fine-tuning for a new robot. LoRA fine-tune needs roughly **27 GB VRAM minimum** (about 72 GB at batch 16). OpenVLA-OFT is the faster, multi-image, bimanual-capable fine-tuning recipe.\n- **Apertus:** small variants (0.5B / 1.5B / 4B) are the realistic candidates for a fully offline on-robot assistant; check each model card for licence and language coverage.\n\n## Compute, sensors and connectivity\n\n| Topic | What is known |\n|---|---|\n| OC Node hardware | Exists per site (\"compute platform designed in-house\"). Chip, RAM, power draw, ports, OS, price: **not public** |\n| Silicon ecosystem | Site shows **Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD** logos; partner logo **HUIXI** (⚠️ unverified which company) |\n| Sensors | None. Cameras / LiDAR come from the robot or other vendors |\n| Connecting a laptop | Not documented. Expect Ethernet / Wi-Fi to whatever box runs the model; confirm on site |\n| Network defaults (IP, ports, credentials) | Not public |\n| Cloud dependency | Their pitch is on-device inference; whether OC Agent itself needs internet during the porting step is ⚠️ unverified — **ask**, venue connectivity may be limited |\n\n**Fallback compute if no OC Node is available:** your team's laptop GPU on the robot's LAN, the robot's own on-board computer, or another vendor's edge board on site (e.g. D-Robotics RDK, per the event vendor list).\n\n## First 60 minutes checklist\n\n1. **Find the OmniCor table (5 min).** Ask what is physically here: OC Node units (how many, which chip), OC Agent access, and which robots on site already run an OmniCor deployment.\n2. **Ask the go / no-go question (5 min):** \"If we give you a fine-tuned pi0.5 / OpenVLA checkpoint or a small LLM tonight, when do we get it running on-board, and what do we call — ROS 2 node, HTTP, library?\" If the answer is longer than about half a day, plan to keep inference on a laptop.\n3. **Pick the model and robot now (10 min).** VLA manipulation (pi0.5 / OpenVLA on a dual-arm or arm platform) or offline language / vision assistant (Apertus / Qwen / Mistral small variant). Write down your budget: target Hz or tokens/s, max latency, power source.\n4. **Stand up the unoptimised baseline in parallel (20 min).** On a laptop GPU: `pip install 'lerobot[pi]'`, accept the PaliGemma licence, load `lerobot/pi05_base`; or load a small Apertus / Qwen instruct model with your usual runtime. This is your fallback and your \"before\" measurement.\n5. **Define the I/O contract (5 min).** Inputs (camera keys, resolution, state vector, instruction string), outputs (action dimension, chunk length, units), transport. Share it with OmniCor and with whoever writes the robot-side code.\n6. **Hello-world inference (10 min).** Feed one recorded frame + instruction, print the action / reply and the **wall-clock latency**. Do not send actions to a real robot yet.\n7. **Verify on hardware (5 min).** Once a deployed version exists: same input, compare output against the baseline and log latency, memory and (if they can show it) power. Only then close the loop on the robot at low speed with the vendor's e-stop in hand.\n\n## Known gotchas and safety\n\n- **Service, not self-serve (most likely).** With a 2–10 person company (LinkedIn figure, ⚠️ unverified) and no public tooling, your throughput depends on their engineers' time. Get in the queue early.\n- **No published numbers.** Any Hz, watt or memory claim you hear on site is unverifiable in advance; measure it yourself before you put it in your pitch.\n- **Optimisation changes behaviour.** Quantised / compiled VLAs can lose precision on fine manipulation. Re-test success rate after optimisation.\n- **A zero-shot VLA will not work on a new robot.** OpenVLA and pi0.5 both need embodiment-specific fine-tuning data; budget teleop + training time (cloud or on-site GPU), which is outside OmniCor's stated scope.\n- **Licences and gating.** PaliGemma tokenizer (pi0.5) is gated and the `lerobot/pi05_base` weights card carries a `gemma` licence tag; OpenVLA weights inherit Llama-2 community licence terms. Download everything **before** you lose connectivity.\n- **pi0.5 dataset stats.** It normalises with quantiles; older LeRobot datasets without `q01` / `q99` fail on the first batch — recompute stats or switch to mean/std normalisation.\n- **E-stop and modes are the robot vendor's.** OmniCor's layer has no safety function. A learned policy must always run behind the robot's own limits, speed caps and emergency stop; keep people (especially for T04 care scenarios) out of reach during first closed-loop runs.\n- **Name confusion.** Not ABB OmniCore (robot controller). Searching \"omnicore\" returns the wrong documentation.\n\n## Community resources\n\n- **OmniCor:** no forum, Discord, GitHub issues or docs. Only channel: the team on site and `contact@omnicorai.com`.\n- **LeRobot:** [docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/index) and [repo](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot) (issues, Discord linked from the repo).\n- **OpenVLA:** [repo](https://github.com/openvla/openvla) issues.\n- **openpi:** [repo](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi) issues.\n- **Apertus:** model cards under [swiss-ai on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai).\n\nResearch note: general web search was unavailable when this guide was written; findings rest on the official site (page and script bundle), direct checks of likely developer URLs, and the profile's earlier research.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) (page text, meta description and site script bundle; only public primary source)\n- [OmnicorAI account on GitHub](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) (no public repositories; ownership unverified)\n- [LeRobot documentation — pi0.5 policy](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05)\n- [LeRobot documentation index](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/index)\n- [LeRobot repository](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot)\n- [OpenVLA repository README](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [pi0.5 base model card in LeRobot (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n- [openpi repository (Physical Intelligence)](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi)\n- [swiss-ai organisation on Hugging Face (Apertus models)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai)\n- [Omnicor AI profile in this vault](Omnicor-AI.md) (company size, people and customer-logo details)\n","Omnicor-AI-Papers-and-Research":"---\ntype: papers\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, papers]\n---\n# Omnicor AI — Papers and Research\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](Omnicor-AI.md) · [Solutions and projects](Omnicor-AI-Solutions-and-Projects.md) · [Developer guide](Omnicor-AI-Developer-Guide.md)\n\n> **Bottom line:** OmniCor AI has **no public research output** as of 2026-11-14 — **0 papers, 0 datasets, 0 models, 0 benchmarks, 0 repositories**. The only research worth reading in connection with it is the third-party work behind the open models its website says it deploys (OpenVLA, pi0.5, Apertus, Qwen, DeepSeek, Mistral). Those papers are listed separately below and are clearly **not** OmniCor work.\n\n## Research footprint\n\n- **Not research-driven in public.** The company presents itself as an edge-AI *deployment* business (OC Agent, OC Node, model optimisation). Its one-page website has no research, technology, blog, news or publications section; the site script bundle contains no link to arXiv, GitHub, Hugging Face or any paper.\n- **Key people and labs: not public.** No founder, CTO or scientific advisor is named on the site, so no author search was possible. Names seen earlier in LinkedIn search snippets (Margueritta Chen, Philippe Chen) are ⚠️ unverified and were **not** used to attribute any paper. No university lab affiliation is stated anywhere.\n- **Open-source posture: none so far.** GitHub account [`OmnicorAI`](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) was created **2026-09-30** and has **0 public repositories** (ownership ⚠️ unverified). No Hugging Face organisation exists under `OmnicorAI`, `omnicor-ai`, `omnicorai`, `OmniCor` or `OmniCorAI` (all return \"not found\").\n- **What it builds on is fully open and well documented.** Every model on its \"models we deploy\" list has a public paper and public weights, so teams can read and reproduce the baseline without OmniCor.\n- **Search status for this doc:** web search **was available** (1 extended query, the full allowance). It returned only the company homepage plus unrelated \"OmniCore\" namesakes. Additional direct checks: company site and script bundle, GitHub API, Hugging Face, and an arXiv full-text query for \"OmniCor\" (**0 results**). All papers listed below were opened on arXiv and the metadata taken from there.\n\n## Company-authored and company-affiliated papers\n\n**None found.** No paper, technical report or preprint authored by OmniCor AI, its (unnamed) founders or an affiliated lab could be identified.\n\n| Paper (title as a link) | Authors / organisation | Venue, year | What it contributes (one line) | Artifacts (code / weights / data links) | Tracks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| — none found — | — | — | — | — | — |\n\nWhat was checked: official site (all six language versions live in one script bundle), GitHub account, Hugging Face org names, arXiv search on the company name, one extended web search. LinkedIn is not readable without login, so staff-level publication lists could not be checked ⚠️ unverified.\n\n## Datasets, models and benchmarks released\n\n**None.** OmniCor AI has not published any dataset, model checkpoint, benchmark, latency table or optimisation toolkit.\n\n| Name | Type | Licence | Link | Useful for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| — none released — | — | — | — | — |\n\nThird-party artifacts you can use instead (the same models the site lists):\n\n| Name | Type | Licence | Link | Useful for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenVLA (`openvla-7b`) | VLA checkpoint + training code | Code MIT; weights inherit Llama-2 licence terms | [repo](https://github.com/openvla/openvla) | Single-arm manipulation baseline; has a ready REST inference server |\n| OpenVLA-OFT | Fine-tuning recipe + checkpoints | See project page | [project page](https://openvla-oft.github.io/) | Faster, chunked, bimanual-capable OpenVLA fine-tunes |\n| pi0.5 in LeRobot (`lerobot/pi05_base`) | VLA checkpoint | LeRobot docs: Apache 2.0; weights card tagged `gemma` | [model card](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base) · [docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05) | Household / mobile-manipulation policies |\n| openpi | Reference code for pi0 / pi0.5 | See repo | [repo](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi) | Original implementation and checkpoints |\n| SmolVLA (in LeRobot) | Small VLA + code + data | Apache 2.0 (LeRobot) | [repo](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot) | VLA that runs on consumer GPU or CPU |\n| Apertus 8B / 70B and small v1.1 variants | Open multilingual LLM | Apache 2.0 | [swiss-ai on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai) | Offline assistant in German / French / Italian / Romansh |\n| BitVLA | 1-bit VLA code + weights | See repo | [code](https://github.com/ustcwhy/BitVLA) · [weights](https://huggingface.co/lxsy/bitvla-bf16) | Memory-constrained on-robot inference |\n| EdgeVLA (EVLA) | Small VLA code + checkpoints | See repo | [code](https://github.com/kscalelabs/evla) | Edge-device VLA inference |\n\n## Third-party papers using their hardware or software\n\n**None found.** No paper mentions OC Agent, OC Node or OmniCor AI as hardware, software or deployment partner. The customer logos on the site (AgiBot, Galbot, \"sudo\") are OmniCor's own claim; no publication from those companies references it.\n\n| Paper (link) | Authors / organisation | Venue, year | Hardware / software used | What it shows | Tracks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| — none found — | — | — | — | — | — |\n\nFor research by the two listed customers that are on site, see [AGIBOT papers and research](../AGIBOT/AGIBOT-Papers-and-Research.md) and the [Galbot profile](../Galbot/Galbot.md).\n\n### Background reading — papers behind the models OmniCor says it deploys (NOT OmniCor work)\n\nAll entries verified on arXiv on 2026-11-14. Listed newest first. OmniCor is not an author, sponsor or named user in any of them.\n\n| Paper (link) | Authors / organisation | Venue, year | What it contributes (one line) | Artifacts | Tracks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Apertus: Democratizing Open and Compliant LLMs for Global Language Environments](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14233) | Project Apertus (A. Hernández-Cano … I. Schlag; 100+ authors; Swiss AI Initiative, EPFL / ETH Zurich / CSCS) | arXiv, 2025 | Fully open LLM at **8B and 70B**, trained on **15T tokens** from **1,800+ languages** (about 40% non-English), compliance-filtered data, anti-memorisation objective | [weights](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai) | T01 T02 T04 |\n| [Real-Time Execution of Action Chunking Flow Policies](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07339) | K. Black, M. Y. Galliker, S. Levine (Physical Intelligence) | NeurIPS 2025 | Real-time chunking (RTC): next action chunk is generated while the current one executes; works on any flow / diffusion VLA **without retraining**; robust to inference delay | [project page](https://pi.website/research/real_time_chunking) | T01 T04 |\n| [BitVLA: 1-bit Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics Manipulation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07530) | H. Wang, C. Xiong, R. Wang, X. Chen | arXiv, 2025 | Ternary-weight VLA that matches OpenVLA-OFT while cutting memory **11.0x** and end-to-end latency **4.4x** | [code](https://github.com/ustcwhy/BitVLA) · [weights](https://huggingface.co/lxsy/bitvla-bf16) | T03 |\n| [SmolVLA: A Vision-Language-Action Model for Affordable and Efficient Robotics](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01844) | M. Shukor, D. Aubakirova, F. Capuano … R. Cadene (Hugging Face) | arXiv, 2025 | Compact VLA trainable on one GPU and deployable on consumer GPU or CPU; asynchronous inference stack; comparable to VLAs **10x larger** | [code](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot) | T01 T03 T04 |\n| [Qwen3 Technical Report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.09388) | A. Yang … Z. Qiu (Qwen team, Alibaba; 60 authors) | arXiv, 2025 | LLM family from **0.6B to 235B** parameters, thinking / non-thinking modes with a thinking budget, **119 languages**, Apache 2.0 | — | T01 T02 T04 |\n| [pi0.5: a Vision-Language-Action Model with Open-World Generalization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.16054) | Physical Intelligence (K. Black, N. Brown … U. Zhilinsky) | arXiv, 2025 | Co-training on multi-robot, web and semantic-subtask data; long-horizon tasks such as cleaning a kitchen or bedroom in **previously unseen homes** | [LeRobot weights](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base) · [openpi](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi) | T01 T04 |\n| [Fine-Tuning Vision-Language-Action Models: Optimizing Speed and Success](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19645) | M. J. Kim, C. Finn, P. Liang (Stanford) | RSS 2025 | OpenVLA-OFT recipe (parallel decoding, action chunking, continuous actions, L1 loss): LIBERO success **76.5% → 97.1%**, action throughput **26x** | [project page](https://openvla-oft.github.io/) | T01 T04 |\n| [DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948) | DeepSeek-AI (D. Guo … Z. Zhang) | Nature 645, 633–638 (2025) | Reasoning LLM trained with reinforcement learning; basis of the distilled small reasoning models often used on edge devices | — | — |\n| [FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for Vision-Language-Action Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09747) | K. Pertsch, K. Stachowicz, B. Ichter … S. Levine (Physical Intelligence) | arXiv, 2025 | DCT-based action tokeniser for high-frequency dexterous data; training up to **5x** faster with pi0 | [project page](https://www.pi.website/research/fast) | — |\n| [pi0: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.24164) | K. Black, N. Brown, D. Driess … U. Zhilinsky (Physical Intelligence) | RSS 2025 | Flow-matching VLA on a pretrained VLM; predecessor of pi0.5 | [openpi](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi) | T01 T04 |\n| [TinyVLA: Towards Fast, Data-Efficient Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotic Manipulation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12514) | J. Wen, Y. Zhu, J. Li … J. Tang | arXiv, 2024 | Compact VLA with a diffusion decoder; faster and more data-efficient than OpenVLA, no pre-training stage | [project page](https://tiny-vla.github.io) | T03 |\n| [EdgeVLA: Efficient Vision-Language-Action Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14049) | P. Budzianowski, W. Maa, M. Freed … B. Bolte (K-Scale Labs) | IROS MoMA3 workshop 2024 (arXiv 2025) | Non-autoregressive end-effector prediction (**7x** faster inference) plus a small language model for edge devices | [code](https://github.com/kscalelabs/evla) | T02 T03 |\n| [OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09246) | M. J. Kim, K. Pertsch, S. Karamcheti … C. Finn (Stanford, UC Berkeley, TRI, Google DeepMind and others) | arXiv, 2024 | **7B**-parameter open VLA trained on **970k** real robot demos; LoRA fine-tuning on consumer GPUs; **quantised serving without loss of success rate** | [code](https://github.com/openvla/openvla) · [project page](https://openvla.github.io/) | T01 T04 |\n| [Mistral 7B](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06825) | A. Q. Jiang, A. Sablayrolles, A. Mensch, C. Bamford and others (Mistral AI) | arXiv, 2023 | The original 7B open LLM from Mistral AI; a common small-model baseline for local inference | — | — |\n| [Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08864) | Open X-Embodiment Collaboration (290+ authors) | arXiv, 2023 | The pooled multi-robot dataset that OpenVLA is trained on | [project page](https://robotics-transformer-x.github.io) | — |\n\nNo paper was found for OpenEuroLLM (also on the site's model list) — not listed.\n\n## Reading list by track\n\nOmniCor has no papers of its own, so every item below is third-party background for the kind of work you would bring to its table: **running an open model on-board, offline, inside a power and memory budget**.\n\n### T01 Autonomous Urban Services\n- [OpenVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09246) — the baseline for language-conditioned pick / place / hand-over; its quantisation result is the evidence that quantised serving can keep success rate (T01.1, T01.3).\n- [OpenVLA-OFT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19645) — if OpenVLA is too slow on your robot, this recipe gives 26x throughput with chunked actions (T01.3).\n- [Real-time chunking](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07339) — how to keep motion smooth when on-board inference is slower than the control loop; no retraining needed (T01.6, T01.7).\n- [Apertus](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14233) and [Qwen3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.09388) — local multilingual LLMs for a guidance / information robot without cloud access (T01.5).\n- [SmolVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01844) — realistic to fine-tune inside 48 h on one GPU.\n\n### T02 Resilient & Safe City\n- **No directly relevant paper found** (nothing from OmniCor; none of the model papers targets rescue, inspection or security).\n- Closest useful background for the \"works when the network is down\" argument (T02.4, T02.7): [EdgeVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14049) for fast edge inference, and [Qwen3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.09388) (0.6B-class models) or [Apertus](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14233) for an offline multilingual operator assistant.\n\n### T03 Sustainable City\n- [BitVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07530) — hard numbers (11x less memory, 4.4x lower latency) for an energy-efficient-inference pitch (T03.3).\n- [SmolVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01844) — a VLA that runs on CPU / consumer GPU; a small sorting policy is feasible in a weekend (T03.1, T03.3).\n- [EdgeVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14049) and [TinyVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12514) — design choices that cut compute per action (T03.3).\n- No paper on waste, water or environmental monitoring is connected to OmniCor.\n\n### T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\n- [pi0.5](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.16054) — the most relevant: household tasks in homes never seen in training (T04.1, T04.6).\n- [Apertus](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14233) — open, compliance-oriented Swiss LLM; the argument for a private on-device assistant in local languages where no patient data leaves the room (T04.5, T04.7).\n- [Real-time chunking](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07339) — smoother motion near people when inference is slow (T04.2).\n- [OpenVLA-OFT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19645) — bimanual, high-frequency fine-tuning recipe for assistive manipulation (T04.1).\n- [SmolVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01844) — lightest path to a working fetch-and-hand-over demo.\n\n## White papers, patents and technical reports\n\nNone found. No white paper, benchmark report, datasheet or patent could be attributed to OmniCor AI. A patent search by company name was not possible within this pass ⚠️ unverified.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) (page and script bundle; no research section, no outbound research links)\n- [OmnicorAI account on GitHub](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) (created 2026-09-30, 0 public repositories; ownership unverified)\n- [Omnicor AI on LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/omnicor-ai/) (not readable without login; nothing taken from it)\n- [arXiv 2509.14233 — Apertus](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14233)\n- [arXiv 2506.07339 — Real-Time Execution of Action Chunking Flow Policies](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07339)\n- [arXiv 2506.07530 — BitVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07530)\n- [arXiv 2506.01844 — SmolVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01844)\n- [arXiv 2505.09388 — Qwen3 Technical Report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.09388)\n- [arXiv 2504.16054 — pi0.5](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.16054)\n- [arXiv 2502.19645 — OpenVLA-OFT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19645)\n- [arXiv 2501.12948 — DeepSeek-R1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948)\n- [arXiv 2501.09747 — FAST](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09747)\n- [arXiv 2410.24164 — pi0](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.24164)\n- [arXiv 2409.12514 — TinyVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12514)\n- [arXiv 2507.14049 — EdgeVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14049)\n- [arXiv 2406.09246 — OpenVLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09246)\n- [arXiv 2310.06825 — Mistral 7B](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06825)\n- [arXiv 2310.08864 — Open X-Embodiment](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08864)\n- [OpenVLA repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla) · [LeRobot pi0.5 docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05) · [pi05_base model card](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base) · [swiss-ai on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai) (licence and artifact details, as verified in the company profile's verification log)\n- [Omnicor AI profile in this vault](Omnicor-AI.md)\n","Omnicor-AI-Products":"---\ntype: products\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, products]\n---\n# Omnicor AI — Product Portfolio\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](Omnicor-AI.md) · [Developer guide](Omnicor-AI-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Omnicor-AI-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> **Read this first (30 seconds).** OmniCor AI is **not a robot vendor**. It is an edge-AI deployment company: you bring a trained model and a target hardware platform, they make the model run on-device. Its entire public catalogue is **four headings on a one-page website** — two named products (OC Agent, OC Node) and two service lines. **No specs, no prices, no datasheets, no docs, no product photos are public.** Everything marked \"not public\" below is a question to ask at their table, not something you can plan against.\n>\n> **Decision rule:** pick OmniCor as a **supporting layer** (on-board / offline inference) on top of another vendor's robot — never as your only platform. Confirm in the first hour what hardware and access they actually have on site.\n\n## Lineup map\n\nTrack relevance: ● strong · ◐ partial · ○ none. Status is ⚠️ unverified for every row: the site lists the item but does not say whether it is shipping, in pilot, or planned.\n\n| Product | Category | Status | Launched | Price (if public) | Positioning (one line) | T01 | T02 | T03 | T04 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **OC Agent** | Software — agentic deployment environment | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site) | not public | not public | Tooling that automates fitting a model to a given edge hardware target | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ |\n| **OC Node** | Hardware — in-house compute platform | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site) | not public | not public | OmniCor's own compute box / board for running models at the edge | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ◐ |\n| **Model optimisation** | Service | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site) | not public | not public | Getting a model inside a power, speed and memory budget | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ |\n| **European delivery** | Service — design and manufacturing in Europe | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site) | not public | not public | \"Designed and built in Europe\" supply and delivery | ○ | ○ | ○ | ○ |\n\nWhy no ● except OC Node / T02: OmniCor contributes inference, not sensing or actuation, so it can only ever be part of a solution. The one place where it could be the core argument is T02, where \"keeps working when the network is down\" is the whole point.\n\n## Products\n\n### OC Agent\n\n**What it is:** the site describes it in three words — an \"agentic deployment environment\". Read together with the rest of the page, it is the software workflow OmniCor uses to take a trained model and produce a deployment for a specific hardware target. Whether it is an internal tool, a hosted service or something you can install is **not public**.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Software (deployment tooling) |\n| Inputs (from site's contact prompt) | Your model + your target hardware |\n| Model families the site says OmniCor deploys | LLMs: Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek · Robot VLAs: OpenVLA, pi0.5 (LeRobot) · \"and others\" |\n| Silicon ecosystems shown on site | Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD (logos only; supported chips / SDK versions not public) |\n| Interface (CLI, GUI, API) | not public |\n| Supported model formats / runtimes | not public |\n| ROS 2 integration | not public ⚠️ unverified |\n| Licence / access model | not public |\n| Docs, SDK, GitHub | none found |\n\n- **Variants / editions:** none published (no EDU or developer edition is mentioned).\n- **Typical applications (per site's deployment domains):** on-board perception and control for embodied-AI robots; multi-sensor inference on L4 autonomous logistics vehicles; rack-side inference on enterprise AI servers.\n- **Hackathon use, if available on site:** port a VLA (OpenVLA, pi0.5) or a local LLM (Apertus, Mistral, Qwen) onto the compute of the robot you chose, so the demo runs without cloud.\n- **Ask before committing:** Can a participant use it directly, or do OmniCor engineers run it for you? How long does one port take — hours or days?\n- **Official page:** [omnicorai.io — What we build](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n### OC Node\n\n**What it is:** a \"compute platform designed in-house\" — OmniCor's own edge compute hardware. That single phrase is the full public description. Form factor (module, board, box, rack unit) is **not public**.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Hardware (compute platform) |\n| Processor / accelerator | not public |\n| AI performance (TOPS, tokens/s, Hz for VLAs) | not public |\n| Memory / storage | not public |\n| Power draw / supply voltage | not public |\n| Interfaces (Ethernet, USB, CAN, camera inputs) | not public |\n| Size / weight / mounting | not public |\n| Operating temperature / IP rating | not public |\n| OS / software stack | not public |\n| Price / availability | not public |\n\n- **Variants:** none published.\n- **Do not assume** it is based on Qualcomm, NVIDIA or AMD silicon: those appear on the site as ecosystem logos for the company, not as the OC Node bill of materials. The site also shows HUIXI as \"our partner\" (probably the Chinese automotive-chip company — ⚠️ unverified); any link to OC Node is speculation.\n- **Typical applications (per site):** the same three domains — robots under a mobile power budget, automotive-grade logistics vehicles, throughput-per-watt servers.\n- **Hackathon use, if units are on site:** a plug-in offline \"brain\" beside a quadruped, humanoid or arm from another vendor — useful where alpine connectivity is weak (T02) or where patient data must stay in the room (T04).\n- **Ask before committing:** How many units are here? Chip, power input, and which ports? Is there a ready image with a model server (HTTP / gRPC / ROS 2 topic) so you can call it from your robot code within an hour?\n- **Official page:** [omnicorai.io — What we build](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n### Model optimisation\n\n**What it is:** a service line, listed as a heading with no description. From the site's framing, it means making a given model meet the constraints of the target device: mobile power budget on robots, thermal and reliability limits on vehicles, throughput per watt on servers.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Service |\n| Techniques used (quantisation, pruning, distillation, compilation) | not public |\n| Published benchmarks (speed-up, accuracy loss, watts) | none |\n| Turnaround time | not public |\n| Price | not public |\n\n- **Typical applications:** shrinking / accelerating an LLM or VLA so it runs on the compute already inside a robot.\n- **Hackathon relevance:** most valuable for T03 arguments (less energy per inference) and for any on-robot VLA demo — but only if it can be done inside 48 h. Ask for a concrete before/after number on a model you care about.\n- **Official page:** [omnicorai.io — What we build](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n### European delivery\n\n**What it is:** the site's fourth item — \"Designed and built in Europe\". A sourcing and delivery statement, not something you can build on during the event.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Service / supply-chain positioning |\n| Manufacturing location, partners, certifications | not public |\n| HQ / legal entity | not public |\n\n- **Hackathon relevance:** none for the build. Useful only as a pitch line (European data-sovereignty and supply-chain angle, alongside European open models such as Apertus, Mistral and OpenEuroLLM).\n- **Official page:** [omnicorai.io — What we build](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n## Accessories and ecosystem\n\nOmniCor publishes no accessories. What surrounds the products, as shown on its site:\n\n| Layer | What the site shows | What it does NOT tell you |\n|---|---|---|\n| Models deployed | Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenVLA, pi0.5 (LeRobot), \"and others\" | Model sizes, versions, achieved speed on any hardware |\n| Silicon ecosystem | Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD | Specific chips or boards supported |\n| Partner | HUIXI | Nature of the partnership ⚠️ unverified |\n| Customers (\"teams shipping with OmniCor AI\") | AgiBot, Galbot, \"sudo\" | Which robots, which models, what volume; identity of \"sudo\" ⚠️ unverified |\n| Deployment domains | Embodied-AI robots · L4 autonomous logistics vehicles · enterprise AI servers | Any named deployment or case study |\n\n**Cross-vendor pairings to explore on site (ideas, not confirmed integrations):**\n\n| Pair with | Why it is plausible | Check first |\n|---|---|---|\n| **AGIBOT** (G1 / G2, A2, X2) | Listed as an OmniCor customer | Is an OmniCor deployment already running on the units here? |\n| **Galbot** (G1) | Listed as an OmniCor customer | Same question; which model? |\n| **Unitree** quadrupeds / humanoids with NVIDIA Jetson-class compute | NVIDIA is in OmniCor's ecosystem list | Whether OmniCor has ever targeted that exact board |\n| **D-Robotics** RDK boards | Alternative edge compute if no OC Node is available | Not in OmniCor's ecosystem list — likely unsupported ⚠️ unverified |\n\n## Legacy and discontinued\n\nNone known. The company has no public product history (website copyright 2026; no earlier announcements found).\n\n## Image credits\n\n**No pictures saved (0 of 4).** The official site contains no product imagery at all: no og:image / twitter:image, and no picture of OC Agent, OC Node or either service. The only images on the site are three small generic illustrations of the deployment domains (robot, logistics vehicle, server), the company logo, and third-party logos. Using any of those as a \"product picture\" would misrepresent what the products look like, so none was stored under a product key. No press kit or Wikimedia Commons media was found.\n\n| file | shows | source URL |\n|---|---|---|\n| — (none) | No official image exists for OC Agent, OC Node, Model optimisation or European delivery | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) checked 2026-11-14 |\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) — the only primary source; one-page site (HTML and its English text strings) read on 2026-11-14\n- [OmniCor AI — \"What we build\" section](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n- [Omnicor AI profile in this pack](Omnicor-AI.md) — earlier research (LinkedIn listing, name disambiguation)\n\nResearch note: the web-search quota for this run was exhausted before any third-party search on OC Agent / OC Node could be made, so this document rests on the official site alone. No independent coverage is cited because none was checked in this pass.\n","Omnicor-AI-Roadmap-and-Strategy":"---\ntype: roadmap\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, roadmap]\n---\n# Omnicor AI — Roadmap and Strategy\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](Omnicor-AI.md) · [Product portfolio](Omnicor-AI-Products.md) · [Developer guide](Omnicor-AI-Developer-Guide.md)\n\n> **Bottom line:** there is **no public roadmap**. No press releases, funding announcements, launch dates, papers or repositories were found. The only primary source is a one-page website. Everything labelled \"inference\" below is a reading of that page, not a company statement. Short doc by design.\n\n## Company trajectory\n\n| Date | Milestone |\n|---|---|\n| Founding date | Not public |\n| 2026 (undated) | Official one-page site live at omnicorai.io, copyright **2026**, in six languages (Chinese, English, French, German, Spanish, Italian). Page markup defaults to Chinese; language is then picked from the visitor's location |\n| 2026 (undated) | Site lists four offerings: **OC Agent**, **OC Node**, model optimisation, European delivery — headings only, no specs or availability |\n| 2026 (undated) | Site shows **AgiBot, Galbot and \"sudo\"** under \"Teams shipping with OmniCor AI\". Scope and volumes not public; \"sudo\" identity ⚠️ unverified |\n| 2026 (undated) | Site shows one partner logo, **HUIXI** (⚠️ unverified which company), and ecosystem logos **Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD** |\n| Funding | Not public. A EUR 3.5M pre-seed figure appeared in a search snippet during profile research — ⚠️ unverified, no primary source |\n| 2026-11-14 to 16 | Listed as \"Omnicor AI\" among hardware / development-platform providers on the public Leukerbad Hackathon event page (linked to its LinkedIn company page). What it brings is not stated; that omnicorai.io is the same company is likely but ⚠️ unverified |\n\n## Current focus projects\n\nWhat the site says they do now (no named projects or case studies):\n\n| Deployment domain | Stated constraint | Evidence level |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Embodied-AI robots** | Perception and control on-board, inside a mobile platform's power budget | Site text + AgiBot / Galbot logos |\n| **L4 autonomous logistics vehicles** | Continuous multi-sensor inference under automotive thermal / reliability limits | Site text only; no named customer |\n| **Enterprise AI servers** | Rack-side inference where throughput per watt sets operating cost | Site text only; no named customer |\n\n- **Model coverage today (per site):** LLMs — Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek; VLAs — OpenVLA, pi0.5; \"and others\".\n- **Team size:** 2–10 (LinkedIn listing, via profile; ⚠️ unverified, page not readable without login). Expect a small number of parallel engagements.\n\n## Announced and upcoming products\n\n| Item | Status | Date | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OC Agent | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site; shipping vs preview not stated) | none given | No version, pricing or access model published |\n| OC Node | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site; shipping vs preview not stated) | none given | No hardware spec published; cannot tell whether units exist in volume |\n| Future products | 🔭 nothing announced | — | No roadmap, teaser or launch date found |\n\nNo item can honestly be marked ✅, 🧪 or 🔭 with a date from public information.\n\n## Strategic direction and markets\n\n**Stated (site):**\n\n- Positioning: make frontier models run at scale on **heterogeneous** hardware; \"bring your AI models to life on any device\".\n- Engagement model: customer supplies trained model + target hardware; OmniCor delivers a deployment tuned for power, speed and memory.\n- \"**Designed and built in Europe**\" is one of four headline items.\n\n**Inference (not company statements):**\n\n- **Bridge play between China and Europe.** Customers shown are Chinese robot makers (AgiBot, Galbot); the page markup defaults to Chinese; the model list mixes Chinese (Qwen, DeepSeek) with European sovereign models (Apertus, Mistral, OpenEuroLLM). A plausible reading: help Chinese embodied-AI hardware ship in Europe with locally acceptable models and on-device (no-cloud) inference, and offer European buyers a Europe-built compute option.\n- **Sovereignty / privacy angle.** Listing Apertus (Swiss) and OpenEuroLLM first-class suggests targeting buyers who need European models and data staying on the device — relevant to healthcare, public sector and industry.\n- **Chip-agnostic with an own box.** Qualcomm / NVIDIA / AMD logos plus OC Node suggests: deploy onto the customer's silicon when it exists, sell OC Node when it does not. HUIXI as sole \"partner\" hints at an automotive-grade chip link for the L4 logistics domain (⚠️ unverified).\n- **Tooling as leverage.** \"Agentic deployment environment\" suggests automating the port-and-optimise loop so a small team can serve many model × hardware pairs.\n\n| Topic | Public status |\n|---|---|\n| HQ / legal entity / country | Not public |\n| Switzerland presence | Not public. Indirect signals only: Apertus on the model list, French / German / Italian site versions, presence at this Swiss hackathon |\n| Partnerships | HUIXI (logo only). No announcement, no terms |\n| Customers | AgiBot, Galbot, \"sudo\" (logos only) |\n| Investors / funding | Not public |\n| Hiring / expansion | Nothing found |\n\n## Research and open-source activity\n\n- **Papers, benchmarks, blog posts:** none found.\n- **Open source:** none. A GitHub account named `OmnicorAI` exists with **0 public repositories** (ownership ⚠️ unverified; not linked from the site). No Hugging Face organisation found under obvious names.\n- **Models / datasets released:** none. All listed models are third-party.\n- **Net:** you cannot evaluate their technology in advance; the hackathon itself is the best chance to see it work.\n\n## What would impress this company at the hackathon\n\n**Inference throughout** — based on how the site frames its value (on-board, power-bounded, heterogeneous hardware, European delivery), not on anything the company has said about the event.\n\nGeneral signals that match their pitch:\n\n- **It runs with the network cable pulled.** A demo that visibly works offline, on the robot or on a small box.\n- **Before / after numbers.** Same model, same task: latency, memory, watts, success rate — baseline vs optimised. Measured on site, shown on one slide.\n- **A real model × hardware pair they can reuse as a reference.** Especially one of their listed models (pi0.5, OpenVLA, Apertus, Mistral, Qwen) on an on-site robot from a listed customer (AgiBot, Galbot).\n- **European / Swiss relevance.** Apertus answering in German, French or Italian on-device fits their \"European delivery\" line and the venue.\n\n| Track | Idea shape that plays to their strengths | Why it would land |\n|---|---|---|\n| T01 Autonomous Urban Services | Delivery / service robot with a VLA or perception stack fully on-board, working across a connectivity dead zone on steep village terrain | \"On-board inside a mobile power budget\" is their first use case, word for word |\n| T02 Resilient & Safe City | Quadruped or sensor node doing local hazard / person detection and spoken guidance when networks are down (storm, avalanche, tunnel) | Offline inference is the whole point; easy to prove by disconnecting |\n| T03 Sustainable City | Quantified energy per inference / per task: same service delivered at a fraction of the watts, or on a smaller battery | Speaks to their throughput-per-watt framing; weaker as a standalone demo, strong as a metric inside another project |\n| T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation | Private on-device assistant for a rehab clinic: multilingual local LLM (Apertus) + vision on a care robot, no patient data leaving the room | Combines privacy, European model and edge deployment — the clearest story for their Europe positioning |\n\n**Likely to count against you (inference):** cloud-API-only demos, claims of on-device performance without measurements, and projects where the edge deployment is cosmetic rather than necessary.\n\nResearch note: general web search was unavailable when this doc was written, so no fresh news scan for 2025–2026 could be run; the \"nothing found\" statements rest on the official site, direct checks of likely official URLs, and the profile's earlier research. Re-check on site.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) (page text, meta description and site script bundle; only public primary source)\n- [OmnicorAI account on GitHub](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) (no public repositories; ownership unverified)\n- [swiss-ai organisation on Hugging Face (Apertus models)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai)\n- [Leukerbad Hackathon 2026 event page (Luma)](https://luma.com/vj13ns00) (vendor list naming Omnicor AI)\n- [Omnicor AI profile in this vault](Omnicor-AI.md) (LinkedIn-derived company size, unverified funding snippet, name caveats)\n","Omnicor-AI-Solutions-and-Projects":"---\ntype: solutions\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, solutions]\n---\n# Omnicor AI — Solutions and Projects Register\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](Omnicor-AI.md) · [Product portfolio](Omnicor-AI-Products.md) · [Papers and research](Omnicor-AI-Papers-and-Research.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Omnicor-AI-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> **Coverage note:** The official site [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) is a **single page** and was walked completely on 2026-11-14: raw HTML (Chinese default text), the full script bundle with all text strings (English, Chinese, French, German, Spanish, Italian — all six say the same thing), every image and logo file. There is **no** sitemap.xml, robots.txt, news, blog, press, case-study or solutions sub-page: every path (`/sitemap.xml`, `/news`, `/blog`, `/llms.txt`) returns the same one page. No separate Chinese-language site exists; `omnicorai.com` (the e-mail domain) and `docs.omnicorai.io` do not resolve. **Web search was available (1 extended query) and returned zero results about this company** — no press, no deployments, no funding news; only unrelated namesakes (ABB OmniCore, Omnicom \"Omni\", Om AI). LinkedIn returned an error without login. No Hugging Face organisation exists under `OmnicorAI` or `omnicor-ai`. **New in this pass:** the partner logo \"HUIXI\" on the OmniCor site carries the Chinese name 辉羲智能, which identifies it as **Huixi Technology**, a Chinese embodied-AI compute-chip company (previously marked unverified). Bottom line: this register is short because the public record is short — nothing was padded.\n\n**How to read this:** OmniCor AI (\"OmniCor\" on its own site) is an **enabler, not a robot vendor**. It has **no named deployment, case study, pilot or dated project** in public. What exists is: 3 deployment domains, 4 \"what we build\" items, a list of models it deploys, 3 customer logos, 1 partner logo, 3 ecosystem logos.\n\n## Solutions catalogue — everything they offer\n\nMaturity is ⚠️ unverified for every row: the site lists each item but never says whether it is shipping, in pilot or planned. No specs, prices or numbers are published for any of them.\n\n| # | Solution / application | What it does | Products used | Maturity | Tracks | Source link |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Embodied-AI robot deployment** (deployment domain 1) | Makes perception and control models run on-board the robot, inside the power budget of a moving platform | OC Agent, OC Node, model optimisation | ⚠️ unverified (customer logos AgiBot, Galbot suggest live work) | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [We serve](https://www.omnicorai.io/#about) |\n| 2 | **L4 autonomous logistics vehicle deployment** (deployment domain 2) | Continuous multi-sensor inference under automotive thermal and reliability limits | OC Agent, OC Node, model optimisation | ⚠️ unverified (no named customer) | T01 | [We serve](https://www.omnicorai.io/#about) |\n| 3 | **Enterprise AI server deployment** (deployment domain 3) | Rack-side inference tuned for throughput per watt (operating cost) | OC Agent, model optimisation | ⚠️ unverified (no named customer) | — | [We serve](https://www.omnicorai.io/#about) |\n| 4 | **OC Agent** | \"Agentic deployment environment\": tooling that automates fitting a trained model to a target hardware platform | — (is the product) | ⚠️ unverified | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech) |\n| 5 | **OC Node** | \"Compute platform designed in-house\": OmniCor's own edge compute hardware (form factor, chip, power not public) | — (is the product) | ⚠️ unverified | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech) |\n| 6 | **Model optimisation** | Service: gets a model inside the power, speed and memory limits of the target device (methods not public) | OC Agent (presumably ⚠️ unverified) | ⚠️ unverified | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech) |\n| 7 | **European delivery** | \"Designed and built in Europe\": sourcing / manufacturing / delivery positioning | OC Node (presumably ⚠️ unverified) | ⚠️ unverified | — | [At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech) |\n| 8 | **On-device language models** (from \"Models we deploy\") | Deploys open LLMs at the edge: **Apertus** (Swiss), **Mistral AI**, **OpenEuroLLM**, **Qwen**, **DeepSeek**, \"and others\" | OC Agent, OC Node, model optimisation | ⚠️ unverified (no version, size or speed given) | T01 T02 T04 | [Models](https://www.omnicorai.io/#models) |\n| 9 | **On-device robot VLA policies** (from \"Models we deploy\") | Deploys vision-language-action models on robot compute: **OpenVLA**, **pi0.5** (LeRobot build) | OC Agent, OC Node, model optimisation | ⚠️ unverified (no Hz, latency or robot named) | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [Models](https://www.omnicorai.io/#models) |\n\nEngagement model for all rows (site wording, paraphrased): you tell them your **trained model** and your **target hardware**; they deliver a deployment that meets your power, speed and memory limits. Contact: contact@omnicorai.com.\n\n### Embodied-AI robot deployment\n\n![Embodied-AI robot deployment](assets/omnicor-ai-sol-embodied-robotics.jpg)\n\n*Generic illustration from the official site — not a photo of a real deployment (none is published).*\n\n- **How it works:** the robot maker supplies model + on-board compute target; OmniCor ports and tunes the model so perception and control run on the robot itself, within its battery / power budget. No cloud link needed at run time.\n- **Who uses it:** site lists **AgiBot**, **Galbot** and **\"sudo\"** as \"teams shipping with OmniCor AI\". Which robots, which models and what volumes: not public. AgiBot and Galbot are both on site at Leukerbad.\n- **Numbers:** none published (no latency, inference rate, watts or accuracy figures).\n- **Track use:** the generic \"brain runs on the robot\" layer for service robots (T01.3, T01.7), rescue / inspection robots when networks fail (T02.1, T02.2, T02.7), sorting manipulators (T03.1) and care robots where data must stay local (T04.1, T04.2, T04.6).\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — We serve](https://www.omnicorai.io/#about)\n\n### L4 autonomous logistics vehicle deployment\n\n![L4 autonomous logistics vehicle deployment](assets/omnicor-ai-sol-l4-logistics.jpg)\n\n*Generic illustration from the official site — not a photo of a real vehicle.*\n\n- **How it works:** continuous multi-sensor (camera / lidar class) inference on vehicle compute, engineered for automotive thermal and reliability constraints.\n- **Who uses it:** no customer named. The only \"partner\" on the site, **Huixi Technology (辉羲智能)**, itself markets compute for \"L4 commercial applications\" (autonomous logistics, mining trucks, cleaning) — a link between the two offerings is plausible but ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Numbers:** none published.\n- **Track use:** autonomous delivery and urban logistics (T01.1), navigation stacks (T01.6). No vehicle is known to be on site; treat as background, not a hackathon platform.\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — We serve](https://www.omnicorai.io/#about)\n\n### OC Agent\n\n- **How it works:** described only as an \"agentic deployment environment\" — software that automates the port-and-tune loop from a trained model to a specific hardware target. Interface (CLI / GUI / API), supported formats and licence: not public.\n- **Who uses it:** not stated; unclear whether participants can use it directly or OmniCor engineers run it for you — ask on site.\n- **Numbers:** none.\n- **Track use:** cross-track enabler — whichever track you pick, this is the path to \"runs on the robot, offline\" (T01.6, T02.6, T03.3, T04.2).\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n### OC Node\n\n- **How it works:** OmniCor's in-house compute platform. Chip, AI performance, memory, power draw, interfaces, size: all not public. Do not assume a specific silicon vendor (Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD are shown as ecosystem logos; Huixi as partner).\n- **Who uses it:** not stated. Whether units are physically at Leukerbad is unknown — first question to ask.\n- **Numbers:** none.\n- **Track use:** a plug-in offline \"brain\" beside another vendor's robot — strongest for T02 (works when the network is down: T02.1, T02.4, T02.7), useful for privacy in T04 (T04.7) and for dead zones in T01 (T01.1, T01.6).\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n### Model optimisation\n\n- **How it works:** service line with a heading only. From the site's framing: fit the model into the device's power, latency and memory envelope. Techniques (quantisation, pruning, distillation, compilation) are not stated.\n- **Who uses it:** the same three domains (robots, L4 vehicles, servers).\n- **Numbers:** no before / after benchmarks published — ask for one on a model you care about.\n- **Track use:** the one OmniCor item that speaks directly to energy: fewer watts per inference (T03.3); also what makes a VLA or LLM fit on robot compute in any track.\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — At a glance](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n\n### On-device language models\n\n- **How it works:** OmniCor says it deploys open LLMs — Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, \"and others\" — onto edge hardware. Model sizes, versions and achieved speed: not public.\n- **Who uses it:** not stated. Three of the five are European models (Apertus is the Swiss open LLM), matching the \"European delivery\" line.\n- **Numbers:** none from OmniCor.\n- **Track use:** offline multilingual guidance and information (T01.5, T01.7), spoken instructions for responders without connectivity (T02.7), private companion / assistant dialogue in care settings (T04.5, T04.7).\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — Models](https://www.omnicorai.io/#models)\n\n### On-device robot VLA policies\n\n- **How it works:** OmniCor lists OpenVLA and pi0.5 (shown as \"PI 0.5 by lerobot\") among the models it deploys — i.e. running a manipulation policy on the robot's own compute instead of an off-board GPU server.\n- **Who uses it:** not stated; AgiBot and Galbot are the only robot makers shown as customers.\n- **Numbers:** none (no control rate, latency or target board published).\n- **Track use:** manipulation by service robots (T01.3), waste sorting and recycling picks (T03.1, T03.6), daily-living and care tasks (T04.1, T04.6), handling in hazardous places (T02.2).\n- Official link: [omnicorai.io — Models](https://www.omnicorai.io/#models)\n\n## Projects register — past and current\n\nNo project in the public record carries a date other than the hackathon itself. Rows marked \"2026 (undated)\" come from the website, whose only date is its 2026 copyright line. Everything except the hackathon row is **OmniCor's own claim via a logo** — no announcement from the other party was found.\n\n| Date | Project / deployment / pilot / partnership | Partner or customer | Location | What happened (one line) | Tracks | Source link |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-11-14 to 16 | Leukerbad Robotics Hackathon 2026 — listed among hardware / development-platform providers | Hackathon organisers | Leukerbad, Switzerland | Named \"Omnicor AI\" on the public event page (linked to its LinkedIn page); what it brings is not stated | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [Luma event page](https://luma.com/vj13ns00) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Customer reference: \"Teams shipping with OmniCor AI\" | **AgiBot** (humanoid / embodied-AI robot maker) | not public | Logo only; robots, models and volume not public | T01 T02 T03 T04 | [Customers](https://www.omnicorai.io/#customers) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Customer reference: \"Teams shipping with OmniCor AI\" | **Galbot** (embodied-AI robot maker) | not public | Logo only; scope not public | T01 T03 T04 | [Customers](https://www.omnicorai.io/#customers) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Customer reference: \"Teams shipping with OmniCor AI\" | **\"sudo\"** (identity ⚠️ unverified — logo has no link) | not public | Logo only | — | [Customers](https://www.omnicorai.io/#customers) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Partnership: shown as \"Our partner\" (the only one) | **Huixi Technology / 辉羲智能** (logo reads \"HUIXI 辉羲智能\") | not public | Logo only; nature and terms of the partnership not public. Huixi's own site does not mention OmniCor | T01 T02 | [Partner section](https://www.omnicorai.io/#partners) · [Huixi official site](https://www.huixi.tech/) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Ecosystem: silicon platforms shown under \"Ecosystem\" | **Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD** | — | Logos only; no specific chips, boards or SDKs named; no partner-programme membership confirmed | — | [Ecosystem section](https://www.omnicorai.io/#partners) |\n\n**Not found (checked, nothing exists in public):** named deployments, case studies, pilots, joint labs, government or EU programmes, competitions, trade-show demos, press releases, funding announcements.\n\n### Partner context: Huixi Technology (not OmniCor projects)\n\nListed because it explains OmniCor's three deployment domains and its AgiBot reference. These are **Huixi's** activities from Huixi's own channels; any role for OmniCor in them is ⚠️ unverified.\n\n| Date | Huixi item | Why it matters for reading OmniCor | Source link |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 (undated) | Positions itself as a full-stack compute platform for embodied AI: chips, modules, software toolchain, foundation model (RPU engine, **RISE** computing platform, **RhinoVLA**) | Same layer OmniCor works at (model-to-silicon deployment) | [Huixi official site](https://www.huixi.tech/) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Huixi Embodied series launched: **R1 Pro, R1 Max, RCM400, RCM500** | Candidate silicon behind an \"in-house\" compute node — inference only, ⚠️ unverified | [Huixi launch article](https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/Qy9gb9fRNZ-Q23lLyBW34Q) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Production case: **AGIBOT G2 powered by Huixi** compute | AgiBot is also OmniCor's first customer logo | [Huixi official site](https://www.huixi.tech/index_en.html) |\n| 2026-09-04 | Strategic cooperation with XBOT (catering robots): adapting XBOT X1 to Huixi Embodied **R1 MAX (500 TOPS-class)**, incl. on-device VLA deployment | Shows the kind of port-and-optimise work the partner does | [Huixi–XBOT article](https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/SCYjPLrSH0RKS31yQ9jqMQ) |\n| 2026 (undated) | Industry applications: embodied robots · spatial intelligence / edge computing · **L4 commercial** (autonomous logistics, mining trucks, cleaning) | Mirrors OmniCor's three domains (robots · L4 logistics vehicles · AI servers) almost one-to-one | [Huixi official site](https://www.huixi.tech/index_en.html) |\n\n**Inference (not a company statement):** the matching domains, the shared AgiBot reference and the single-partner slot suggest OmniCor acts as a Europe-facing delivery and deployment arm around Huixi-class compute. Confirm on site before using this in a pitch.\n\n## Future projects and announced plans\n\n| Announced | Planned for | What | Confidence (official / reported / inferred) | Tracks | Source link |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| — | — | **Nothing announced.** No roadmap, launch date, product teaser, funding round, expansion or hiring plan exists in public (site, search, GitHub all checked 2026-11-14) | official (absence confirmed on the site) | — | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n\nOpen points that look like \"future\" but are really unknowns — ask at their table:\n\n- Are **OC Agent** and **OC Node** available today (✅), in pilot (🧪) or planned (🔭)? The site does not say.\n- Will there be public docs, an SDK or a self-serve version of OC Agent?\n- Which further models hide behind \"and others\"?\n\n## Solutions by track — quick index\n\nEvery entry is an **enabling layer** on top of another vendor's robot. Nothing below is a ready-made track solution.\n\n### T01 Autonomous Urban Services\n\n- **Embodied-AI robot deployment** — on-board perception / control for public-space service robots · T01.3, T01.7\n- **On-device language models** (Apertus, Mistral, Qwen …) — offline multilingual guidance and information · T01.5, T01.7\n- **L4 autonomous logistics vehicle deployment** — delivery and logistics vehicles, on-vehicle multi-sensor inference · T01.1, T01.6\n- **On-device robot VLA policies** (OpenVLA, pi0.5) — manipulation for service tasks without a cloud GPU · T01.3, T01.2\n- **OC Node** — offline compute for connectivity dead zones · T01.1, T01.6\n- **OC Agent** / **Model optimisation** — porting a navigation or perception model to robot compute · T01.6, T01.4\n- Customer references **AgiBot**, **Galbot** — both have robots on site · T01.3\n\n### T02 Resilient & Safe City\n\n- **OC Node** — inference that keeps working when networks are down · T02.1, T02.4, T02.7\n- **Embodied-AI robot deployment** — on-board hazard / person detection on rescue or inspection robots · T02.1, T02.2, T02.6\n- **On-device language models** — spoken guidance for responders with no connectivity · T02.7\n- **Model optimisation** — fitting detection models on small, battery-powered sensor nodes · T02.3, T02.5\n- **On-device robot VLA policies** — remote-site manipulation without a link to a server · T02.2\n- **OC Agent** — fast re-porting of a model to whichever robot is available · T02.6\n\n### T03 Sustainable City\n\n- **Model optimisation** — fewer watts per inference; measurable energy saving per task · T03.3\n- **On-device robot VLA policies** — sorting / picking policies running on the manipulator's own compute · T03.1, T03.6\n- **Embodied-AI robot deployment** — on-board perception for waste-sorting or monitoring robots · T03.1, T03.2\n- **OC Node** / **OC Agent** — local processing of environmental sensor data instead of cloud upload · T03.7 (weak fit)\n- Enterprise AI server deployment (throughput per watt) is energy-related but not robotic — background only\n\n### T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\n\n- **On-device language models** (Apertus, Mistral …) — private, multilingual companion and assistant dialogue; no patient data leaves the room · T04.5, T04.7\n- **Embodied-AI robot deployment** — on-board perception / control for care robots · T04.1, T04.2, T04.6\n- **On-device robot VLA policies** — daily-living manipulation (fetch, hand over, tidy) on the robot · T04.1, T04.6\n- **OC Node** — local compute for vision-based monitoring with data kept on site · T04.7\n- **Model optimisation** / **OC Agent** — making pose-estimation or speech models fit on small care devices · T04.3, T04.4 (indirect)\n- Customer references **AgiBot**, **Galbot** — candidate care-robot platforms on site · T04.2\n\n## What they do NOT offer\n\n- **No robot, arm, gripper, sensor or vehicle of their own.** Every track project needs another vendor's hardware.\n- **No ready-made application** for any track: no delivery, cleaning, inspection, rescue, waste-sorting, monitoring, care or rehabilitation solution.\n- **No public SDK, API, docs, GitHub code or simulation assets.** A GitHub account `OmnicorAI` exists but shows no public code (ownership ⚠️ unverified); no Hugging Face organisation.\n- **No models or datasets of their own** — all listed models are third-party open models.\n- **No published specs or benchmarks** for OC Node or any deployment (no TOPS, watts, latency, control rate).\n- **No named deployment or case study** — customer and partner evidence is logos only.\n- **No stated ROS 2 integration, navigation stack, fleet management or teleoperation.**\n- **No healthcare or safety certifications stated** (medical-device, functional-safety, automotive grade) — relevant for T04 and T02 pitches.\n- **No public pricing, availability or support terms.**\n\n## Image credits\n\nOnly 2 pictures saved. The official site has no deployment photos, no product photos and no social-preview image; its only solution imagery is three small generic illustrations of the deployment domains (the server one was skipped: no track relevance).\n\n| file | shows | source URL |\n|---|---|---|\n| assets/omnicor-ai-sol-embodied-robotics.jpg | Generic site illustration for the \"Embodied AI Robotics\" deployment domain (cartoon robot; not a real deployment) | [omnicorai.io/illustrations/robotics.png](https://www.omnicorai.io/illustrations/robotics.png) |\n| assets/omnicor-ai-sol-l4-logistics.jpg | Generic site illustration for the \"L4 Autonomous Logistics Vehicles\" deployment domain (cartoon vehicle; not a real vehicle) | [omnicorai.io/illustrations/logistics.png](https://www.omnicorai.io/illustrations/logistics.png) |\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) — full page HTML, script bundle with all six language versions, illustrations and logo files (read 2026-11-14)\n- [OmniCor AI — We serve (deployment domains)](https://www.omnicorai.io/#about)\n- [OmniCor AI — At a glance (what we build)](https://www.omnicorai.io/#tech)\n- [OmniCor AI — Models we deploy](https://www.omnicorai.io/#models)\n- [OmniCor AI — Customers](https://www.omnicorai.io/#customers)\n- [OmniCor AI — Partner and ecosystem](https://www.omnicorai.io/#partners)\n- [OmniCor AI — partner logo file showing \"HUIXI 辉羲智能\"](https://www.omnicorai.io/logos/huixi.png)\n- [Leukerbad Hackathon 2026 event page (Luma)](https://luma.com/vj13ns00) — lists \"Omnicor AI\" among hardware / development-platform providers\n- [Huixi Technology official site (Chinese)](https://www.huixi.tech/) and [English version](https://www.huixi.tech/index_en.html) — partner identity, product names, industry applications, AGIBOT G2 production case\n- [Huixi: strategic cooperation with XBOT (2026-09-04)](https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/SCYjPLrSH0RKS31yQ9jqMQ) — official Huixi WeChat article\n- [Huixi: Huixi Embodied series launch](https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/Qy9gb9fRNZ-Q23lLyBW34Q) — official Huixi WeChat article (title and summary read; date not confirmed)\n- [OmnicorAI account on GitHub](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) — account page exists; no public code seen; ownership unverified\n- [Omnicor AI on LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/omnicor-ai/) — not readable without login (error returned); nothing taken from it in this pass\n- Web search (1 extended query on company name, OC Agent, OC Node, customers and partner, 2026 news): no relevant result. Unrelated hits only, e.g. [TechNode on Om AI](https://technode.com/2026/05/27/from-video-understanding-to-edge-deployment-om-ai-targets-real-world-ai/) — a different company, not used\n","Omnicor-AI":"---\ntype: company\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nhq: \"not public (Europe; site says designed and built in Europe)\"\nfounded: unknown\nwebsite: \"https://www.omnicorai.io/\"\ncategories: [software, compute]\non_site_guess: \"Engineers plus an edge-deployment service/toolchain (OC Agent) and possibly an OC Node compute box, to get VLA / LLM models running on-board other vendors' robots\"\nfit_t01: 3\nfit_t02: 3\nfit_t03: 2\nfit_t04: 3\nidentity_confidence: medium\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, company]\n---\n# Omnicor AI\n\n> **One-liner:** OmniCor AI is a very small European edge-AI deployment company: it takes a trained model (LLM, VLA) plus a target hardware platform and makes the model run on-device within power, speed and memory limits, using an agentic deployment environment (OC Agent) and an in-house compute platform (OC Node).\n> **Why it matters at Leukerbad:** It is not a robot vendor but an enabler: if your idea needs a VLA or a language model running **on the robot** (no cloud, weak alpine connectivity, patient privacy), this is the team to ask. Its site lists **AgiBot** and **Galbot** — both on site — as teams shipping with it.\n\n**Public information is very thin.** Everything below comes from a single one-page official website (no product pages, no specs, no docs, no prices) plus the LinkedIn company listing. Treat all capability detail as \"ask on site\".\n\n## Snapshot\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| HQ | Not public. Site states \"designed and built in Europe\"; site is offered in Chinese, English, French, German, Spanish, Italian |\n| Founded | Unknown (website copyright 2026) |\n| Founders / CEO | Not public. People associated on LinkedIn per search snippets: Margueritta Chen, Philippe Chen (names and roles ⚠️ unverified; LinkedIn page not readable without login) |\n| Size and funding | 2–10 employees (LinkedIn listing, ⚠️ unverified: page not readable without login). Funding: not public. A search snippet attributed a EUR 3.5M pre-seed to a related LinkedIn profile — ⚠️ unverified, no primary source found, do not rely on it |\n| Core business | Deploying and optimising AI models on heterogeneous edge hardware: embodied-AI robots, L4 autonomous logistics vehicles, enterprise AI servers |\n| Website | https://www.omnicorai.io/ (contact: contact@omnicorai.com) |\n| Docs / developer portal | Not public (none found) |\n| GitHub | No public code. A GitHub account [`OmnicorAI`](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) exists with **0 public repositories**; not linked from the official site, ownership ⚠️ unverified |\n\nName caveats: written \"OmniCor AI\" on its own site. Not ABB's OmniCore controller, not \"OmniCore AI\" (several unrelated firms), not Omnicor (South African assessment company), not omnicor.tech (unrelated \"launching soon\" placeholder).\n\n## Portfolio at a glance\n\nThe website lists four \"what we build\" items, with headings only — no specs published.\n\n| Product | Type | Status | Key specs | Price | Img key |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OC Agent | Software — agentic deployment environment (automates fitting a model to target hardware) | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site, availability not stated) | Not public | Not public | omnicor-ai-oc-agent |\n| OC Node | Hardware — compute platform designed in-house | ⚠️ unverified (listed on site, availability not stated) | Not public (chip, TOPS, power, I/O all unstated) | Not public | omnicor-ai-oc-node |\n| Model optimisation | Service — optimising models for power / latency / memory budgets | ⚠️ unverified | Not public | Not public | omnicor-ai-model-optimization |\n| European delivery | Service — design and manufacturing in Europe | ⚠️ unverified | Not public | Not public | omnicor-ai-europe-delivery |\n\n**Models the site says it deploys:** Apertus (Swiss open LLM), Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenVLA, pi0.5 (LeRobot), \"and more\".\n**Silicon ecosystem logos:** Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD. **Partner logo:** HUIXI (most likely the Chinese automotive-chip company — ⚠️ unverified).\n\n## What they will most likely bring to the hackathon\n\n**Best guess, not confirmed:**\n\n- **People, not robots.** A 2–10 person deployment company most plausibly brings engineers who help teams run models on-board hardware from other vendors.\n- **Possibly OC Node units** as a plug-in compute box, and **OC Agent** access as the workflow for porting a model. Neither is confirmed; no public spec or SDK exists to prepare against.\n- **Most natural pairing:** AgiBot and Galbot robots (both shown as customers on the OmniCor site), and NVIDIA / Qualcomm / AMD-based compute, e.g. Jetson-class boards.\n- **Reasoning:** the site's first use case is embodied-AI robots where perception and control must run on-board within a mobile power budget, and the deployed-model list includes two robot VLAs (OpenVLA, pi0.5).\n\n## Developer platform (summary)\n\n- **SDK / languages:** not public. No docs portal, no GitHub org, no package names found.\n- **Engagement model (from site):** you provide the trained model and the target hardware; they build the deployment to fit power, speed and memory limits. This reads as a service plus tooling, not a self-serve SDK.\n- **Control level:** not a robot-control stack. It sits at the inference layer (model runtime / optimisation), under your policy and above the chip.\n- **ROS 2:** not stated ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Simulation assets, teleoperation, datasets:** none public.\n- **Pretrained models:** no models of its own are published; it deploys third-party open models (Apertus, Mistral, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenVLA, pi0.5).\n- **On-board compute:** OC Node (in-house); also targets Qualcomm, NVIDIA and AMD platforms per the ecosystem logos.\n- **Questions to ask on site:**\n  1. What is physically here — OC Node units? How many, what chip, power draw, interfaces (USB / Ethernet / CAN)?\n  2. Which robots on site already have a working OmniCor deployment (AgiBot? Galbot?) and which models?\n  3. Inference rate and latency for OpenVLA / pi0.5 on your hardware?\n  4. How long to port a new or fine-tuned model — hours or days? Can it be done inside 48 h?\n  5. Is there a ROS 2 node or simple HTTP / gRPC API to call the model?\n  6. Can a local LLM (Apertus, Mistral, Qwen) run fully offline with speech in German / French?\n\n## Current projects, deployments and customers\n\n- 2026 (website, undated): **AgiBot, Galbot and \"sudo\"** shown as teams shipping with OmniCor AI. Scope, products and volumes not public; identity of \"sudo\" ⚠️ unverified. [Source](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- 2026 (website, undated): three stated deployment domains — embodied-AI robots, L4 autonomous logistics vehicles, enterprise (rack-side) AI servers. No named deployments or case studies. [Source](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- 2026-11: the public Leukerbad Hackathon event page lists **\"Omnicor AI\"** among its hardware / development-platform providers (linked to the LinkedIn company page). What it brings is not stated. [Source](https://luma.com/vj13ns00)\n- No press coverage or press releases found as of 2026-11-14.\n\n## Roadmap and future direction\n\n- Nothing announced publicly: no roadmap, launch dates, funding announcement or expansion plans found.\n- Readable direction from the site (inference, not statement): a Europe-based delivery arm for edge AI that bridges Chinese robot makers and open models (including European ones such as Apertus, Mistral, OpenEuroLLM) with Qualcomm / NVIDIA / AMD silicon and its own compute node.\n\n## Challenge fit\n\nScores reflect a supporting, multi-vendor role and the lack of public tooling; raise them by one if the team confirms hardware and a fast porting path on site.\n\n| Track | Fit 0–5 | Why (one line) | Best products | Go deeper |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 Autonomous Urban Services | 3 | On-board VLA / perception for delivery or service robots where mountain connectivity is patchy; needs another vendor's robot | OC Agent, OC Node | [T01 brief](T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/Omnicor-AI-T01-1-Brief.md) |\n| T02 Resilient & Safe City | 3 | Offline inference is the point when networks fail (avalanche, storm, tunnel); local detection on quadrupeds or sensors | OC Node, model optimisation | [T02 brief](T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/Omnicor-AI-T02-1-Brief.md) |\n| T03 Sustainable City | 2 | Lower inference power per task is a real but indirect contribution; hard to demo as the core of a project | Model optimisation | [T03 brief](T03-Sustainable-City/Omnicor-AI-T03-1-Brief.md) |\n| T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation | 3 | Private, on-device language and vision models (no patient data leaving the clinic), multilingual local LLMs incl. Swiss Apertus | OC Agent, OC Node | [T04 brief](T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/Omnicor-AI-T04-1-Brief.md) |\n\n## Related docs\n- [Product portfolio](Omnicor-AI-Products.md) · [Developer guide](Omnicor-AI-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Omnicor-AI-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n- [Start here — all companies and tracks](../../00-START-HERE.md)\n\n## Verification log (2026-11-14)\n\nFact-check pass over all 16 docs in this folder. General web search was unavailable in this pass (budget exhausted), so every check is a direct read of an official page. 20 claims checked: 12 confirmed, 5 corrected, 3 unverified.\n\n- ✔ confirmed — Site lists exactly four offerings (OC Agent, OC Node, model optimisation, European delivery) with no specs, prices or docs. [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- ✔ confirmed — \"Models we deploy\" logos: Apertus, Mistral AI, Open Euro LLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenVLA, PI 0.5 (by LeRobot). [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- ✔ confirmed — Customer logos AgiBot, Galbot and \"sudo\" are on the site. This is OmniCor's own claim only; nothing from AgiBot or Galbot confirms it. [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/#customers)\n- ✔ confirmed — Partner logo HUIXI; ecosystem logos Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD (logos only, no chips named). [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/#partners)\n- ⚠ unverified — Which company \"HUIXI\" is (logo has no link or description). [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/#partners)\n- ✔ confirmed — Three deployment domains: embodied-AI robots, L4 autonomous logistics vehicles, enterprise AI servers. [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- ✔ confirmed — Six site languages, page markup defaults to Chinese with location-based language pick, copyright 2026, contact@omnicorai.com. [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- ✎ corrected — Profile said GitHub \"none found\"; a GitHub account `OmnicorAI` exists with 0 public repositories (ownership unverified), as the developer guide already stated. [github.com/OmnicorAI](https://github.com/OmnicorAI)\n- ✎ corrected — Profile and roadmap said the public event page does not name Omnicor; it does list \"Omnicor AI\" among hardware / development-platform providers, linked to its LinkedIn page. [Luma event page](https://luma.com/vj13ns00)\n- ⚠ unverified — LinkedIn details (2–10 employees, associated people). Page returns an error without login. [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/omnicor-ai/)\n- ⚠ unverified — EUR 3.5M pre-seed figure: no primary source; do not use. [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) (no funding information on the site)\n- ✎ corrected — OpenVLA size was \"7B\" in the developer guide and \"7.5B\" elsewhere; harmonised to \"`openvla-7b` checkpoint, about 7.5B parameters\". Confirmed unchanged: 970K trajectories, DINOv2 + SigLIP + Llama-2, `vla-scripts/deploy.py` REST server, LoRA about 27 GB minimum (about 72 GB at batch 16), code MIT. [OpenVLA repo](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- ✔ confirmed — pi0.5 in LeRobot: `pip install 'lerobot[pi]'`, `policy.type=pi05`, checkpoints `lerobot/pi05_base` and `lerobot/pi05_libero_base`, gated PaliGemma tokenizer, reference fine-tune sized for one 80 GB GPU, default chunk size 50, `train_expert_only`, quantile stats (`q01` / `q99`). [LeRobot pi0.5 docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05)\n- ✎ corrected — pi0.5 licence was \"Apache 2.0\" in some docs and \"gemma\" in another. Both sources exist: LeRobot docs state Apache 2.0, the `lerobot/pi05_base` weights card is tagged `gemma`. All docs now say both. [pi05_base model card](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n- ✔ confirmed — `lerobot/pi05_base`: about 4B parameters, flow-matching action head only. [pi05_base model card](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n- ✎ corrected — Apertus language count was \"1,811\" in two docs and \"over 1,000\" in a third; the model card gives both (1,811 natively supported). Harmonised. Confirmed unchanged: 8B and 70B sizes, Apache-2.0, context up to 65,536 tokens, Transformers / vLLM / SGLang / MLX. [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 card](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- ✔ confirmed — Small Apertus v1.1 variants (0.5B / 1.5B / 4B, with Instruct versions) and multimodal Apertus v1.5 (8B, 70B) exist on the Hub. [swiss-ai on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai)\n- ✔ confirmed — Smallest YOLO26 detector about 2.4M parameters; AGPL-3.0; ONNX / TensorRT export. [Ultralytics repo](https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics)\n- ✔ confirmed — MediaPipe Pose Landmarker: 33 landmarks, world coordinates in metres, built for on-device real-time use. [MediaPipe guide](https://developers.google.com/edge/mediapipe/solutions/vision/pose_landmarker)\n- ✔ confirmed — LeRobot is Apache-2.0 and ships ACT, SmolVLA and pi0.5 with `lerobot-record` / `lerobot-train`; Piper TTS is GPL-3.0 and local. [LeRobot repo](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot) · [Piper repo](https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl)\n\nStructural fixes in the same pass: 16 image lines removed from the four track \"Products and Solutions\" docs (the assets folder is empty; the site has no product imagery); a Sources section added to the T01 brief; briefs now end with Sources. Not re-checked: licences of whisper.cpp, CodeCarbon, TACO and TrashNet.\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI on LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/omnicor-ai/) (company listing; page not readable without login, details from starting notes and search snippets)\n- [Leukerbad Hackathon 2026 event page (Luma)](https://luma.com/vj13ns00) (lists \"Omnicor AI\" among hardware / development-platform providers; no detail on what it brings)\n- [OmnicorAI account on GitHub](https://github.com/OmnicorAI) (no public repositories; ownership unverified)\n- [OpenVLA repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla), [LeRobot pi0.5 docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05), [pi05_base model card](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base), [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509), [swiss-ai on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai), [Ultralytics repository](https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics), [MediaPipe Pose Landmarker guide](https://developers.google.com/edge/mediapipe/solutions/vision/pose_landmarker), [LeRobot repository](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot), [Piper TTS repository](https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl) (third-party facts checked in the verification log)\n","Omnicor-AI-T01-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T01\nchallenge_title: \"Autonomous Urban Services\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, brief]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T01 Autonomous Urban Services — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — Not a robot: an on-device inference enabler that makes another vendor's delivery or service robot work without cloud; useful as one part of a multi-vendor build, and only if the team has tooling or hardware on site (drops to 2/5 if not).\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **OC Agent** (agentic deployment environment) plus **OC Node** (in-house compute), both ⚠️ unverified: no specs, SDK or docs are public |\n| Strongest proof point | None dated or detailed. The official site (2026) lists \"L4 Autonomous Logistics Vehicles\" and embodied-AI robots as deployment domains and shows **AgiBot** and **Galbot** logos as customers; no case study, no numbers |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Offline multilingual concierge / wayfinding robot: a local LLM (e.g. Apertus 8B) running on-board a quadruped or wheeled humanoid, demo continues with Wi-Fi unplugged |\n| Biggest risk | Unknown what is physically on site and whether a model can be ported inside 48 h; no self-serve SDK to prepare against |\n| Pairs well with | Galbot G1, AGIBOT G1 / G2 (site-listed customers) · Unitree Go2 / B2 · D-Robotics RDK boards · Binabik AI |\n\n## Why\n- **Connectivity is the local pain point.** Steep valley, snow, basements and lifts: a delivery or guide robot that depends on cloud inference stalls. On-device models are the fix Omnicor sells.\n- **Stated domain match.** \"L4 autonomous logistics vehicles\" (continuous multi-sensor inference) is the closest of its three domains to T01, though unproven publicly.\n- **Model list fits service robots.** It says it deploys OpenVLA and pi0.5 (manipulation at pickup / hand-over) and Apertus, Mistral, Qwen (dialogue, task parsing).\n- **Existing ties to two vendors on site** (AgiBot, Galbot) may shorten integration ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Privacy angle:** guest speech and camera data never leave the robot.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams that need a navigation stack, a robot body, sensors or payload: Omnicor provides none of these.\n- Teams wanting documented APIs today: nothing is public; everything depends on the engineers on site.\n- Ideas where a laptop GPU or cloud API is good enough; then the edge layer adds work without visible value.\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T01-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/T01-0-Track-Overview.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (this repo)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n","Omnicor-AI-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, products]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T01 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T01-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** Omnicor AI (written \"OmniCor AI\" on its site) publishes a single one-page website with headings only. There are **no specs, prices, docs, SDK, GitHub or case studies**. Every \"not public\" below is literal. The company sells the inference layer (getting a model to run on a given chip within power, speed and memory limits); it does not make robots, sensors or a navigation stack. Research note: only the official site and model pages could be checked in this pass; no press coverage was found by the profile research either.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### OC Agent\n\n**Role in T01:** the tool (or workflow) that fits a model to the compute already inside a delivery or service robot, so task understanding, dialogue or a manipulation policy runs on-board and keeps working when the network drops.\n\n| Track-relevant item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| What it is (site wording) | \"Agentic deployment environment\" |\n| Input it needs | Your trained model plus your target hardware (site contact prompt) |\n| Supported target silicon | Not public. Ecosystem logos shown: Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD |\n| Model types it claims to deploy | LLMs (Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek) and robot VLAs (OpenVLA, pi0.5 via LeRobot) |\n| Porting time per model | Not public |\n| Latency / throughput gains | Not public |\n| ROS 2 integration | Not public ⚠️ unverified |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified (listed on site; no release, licence or pricing information). Hackathon access: ask on site.\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Assume it is operated by or with Omnicor engineers, not self-serve. It controls *how a model runs* (runtime, precision, memory footprint), not the robot.\n\n### OC Node\n\n**Role in T01:** a possible drop-in compute box to mount on a robot or cart that lacks enough on-board compute, or a local \"town edge server\" that several robots call over the venue LAN instead of the internet.\n\n| Track-relevant item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| What it is (site wording) | \"Compute platform designed in-house\" |\n| Chip / accelerator | Not public |\n| Compute (TOPS / GPU memory) | Not public |\n| Power draw, supply voltage | Not public |\n| Size, weight, mounting | Not public |\n| I/O (Ethernet, USB, CAN, camera inputs) | Not public |\n| Ingress / temperature rating | Not public |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified. Whether any unit is at Leukerbad is unknown.\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Before planning around it, confirm OS, how you log in, and how a model is called (ROS 2 topic, HTTP, gRPC).\n\n### Model optimisation\n\n**Role in T01:** shrinking a model so a small robot can afford it: faster responses for a concierge robot, longer battery life on a delivery round, a VLA that reaches a usable control rate on embedded compute.\n\n| Track-relevant item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Stated optimisation targets | Power, speed and memory limits of the target device (site framing) |\n| Methods (quantisation, distillation, compilation) | Not public |\n| Published benchmarks | None |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified; reads as a service. Price not public.\n- **Developer interface:** none public. You hand over a model and constraints.\n\n### European delivery\n\n**Role in T01:** not usable in a 48-hour build. Relevant only for the pitch: a municipality or hotel buying service robots may prefer compute that is \"designed and built in Europe\" (site wording) and data that stays on the device.\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified. No factory, location or partner named.\n\n## What Omnicor AI already does in this domain\n\nShort answer: **nothing verifiable**. No dated deployment, pilot, demo or customer story in urban services, delivery or logistics is public.\n\n| Date | Item | Relevance to T01 | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 (site, undated) | \"L4 Autonomous Logistics Vehicles\" listed as a deployment domain, described as continuous multi-sensor inference under automotive thermal and reliability constraints | Closest claim to autonomous delivery. No vehicle maker, city, fleet size or date given | [Official site](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| 2026 (site, undated) | \"Embodied AI Robotics\" listed as a domain: perception and control on-board within a moving platform's power budget | Applies to service and delivery robots generally | [Official site](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| 2026 (site, undated) | Customer logos: AgiBot, Galbot, \"sudo\" | Both robot makers are on site; scope of the work is not public; \"sudo\" not identified ⚠️ unverified | [Official site](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| 2026 (site, undated) | Partner logo HUIXI; ecosystem logos Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD | HUIXI is probably the Chinese automotive-chip firm, which would fit the L4 vehicle claim ⚠️ unverified | [Official site](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced:** nothing. No roadmap, release dates, events or funding news found.\n- **Inference (not a statement by the company):** the pairing of an automotive-chip partner logo with the L4 logistics domain suggests work on vehicle-grade inference for delivery vehicles; the AgiBot and Galbot logos plus OpenVLA / pi0.5 suggest on-board VLA deployment for mobile manipulators. Either would be relevant to T01, neither is confirmed.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\nOmnicor publishes no code, models or datasets. The shortcuts are the third-party open models its site says it deploys; you can start on these immediately on a laptop GPU and ask Omnicor to move them on-device.\n\n| Asset | What it gives a T01 build | Facts checked | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Apertus 8B Instruct (Swiss AI Initiative) | Local multilingual dialogue and task parsing for concierge, wayfinding, order taking | **8B** and 70B sizes · Apache-2.0 · 1,811 supported languages stated · served via Transformers, vLLM (OpenAI-compatible API), SGLang, MLX; community quantised builds exist for llama.cpp / Ollama | ✅ |\n| OpenVLA | Manipulation policy for pick-up and hand-over steps (image + instruction in, 7-DoF action out) | `openvla-7b` checkpoint, about **7.5B** parameters · code MIT, weights inherit Llama-2 community licence terms · LoRA fine-tuning script provided (about 27 GB GPU memory minimum stated for LoRA) · inference rate on embedded hardware not stated | ✅ |\n| pi0.5 in LeRobot (`lerobot/pi05_base`) | Alternative VLA with multi-camera input, loadable through LeRobot | About **4B** parameters · weights card tagged `gemma` licence (LeRobot docs state Apache 2.0 for the implementation) · LeRobot port has the flow-matching action head only · fine-tune with `lerobot-train` | ✅ |\n| Mistral, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenEuroLLM | Other LLM options the site lists | Not checked in this pass | ⚠️ unverified which variants Omnicor supports |\n\nPractical notes:\n- **VLAs do not navigate.** OpenVLA and pi0.5 are arm-manipulation policies. Street and corridor navigation must come from the robot vendor's own stack or a standard ROS 2 navigation setup.\n- **Fine-tuning a VLA needs data and a large GPU**; plan on pretrained behaviour or a very narrow task within 48 hours.\n- No Omnicor datasets, simulation assets or sample apps exist publicly.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No robot, no sensors, no navigation, no payload.** A T01 project needs a body from another vendor; Omnicor is at most one layer in it.\n- **Zero public technical detail.** No latency, power or memory numbers, so you cannot size a design before talking to the team.\n- **No self-serve path.** No SDK, docs or repo: progress depends on engineer availability during the event.\n- **Porting risk.** Whether a model can be optimised for a specific robot's computer within 48 hours is unknown.\n- **Value is easy to miss in a demo.** \"It runs locally\" is invisible unless you stage it (unplug the network, show power or latency on screen).\n- **Proof points are logos only.** Do not cite AgiBot, Galbot or L4 logistics work in a pitch as demonstrated deployments.\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) (domains, \"what we build\" list, model, customer and ecosystem logos)\n- [OpenVLA repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [pi0.5 base model in LeRobot (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n- [Apertus 8B Instruct model card (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (internal)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n","Omnicor-AI-T01-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, playbook]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T01 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Ground rules for every concept below**\n- Omnicor AI supplies the on-device inference layer only. Each concept needs a robot from another vendor, and that vendor's SDK does the moving.\n- No Omnicor SDK, spec or doc is public. **Hour 0 task: talk to the Omnicor engineers** (questions below) and learn what OC Agent / OC Node can do here. Until then, develop on a laptop GPU with the same open models so you are never blocked.\n- Make the edge value visible: every demo should include an **\"unplug the network\" moment** and, if possible, a live latency or power readout.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Offline alpine concierge and guide (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Spa guests and elderly visitors need directions, opening hours and escort between hotel, baths and clinic. A robot that answers in German, French, Italian and English and leads the way, with no cloud dependency and no voice data leaving the device.\n- **Hardware:** OC Agent-deployed LLM on the robot's own computer, or an OC Node strapped on (both ⚠️ unverified) · body: Unitree Go2 or a wheeled service humanoid (UniX AI Wanda, Spirit AI Moz1) · microphone and speaker.\n- **Architecture / stack:** speech-to-text (local) → local LLM such as Apertus 8B Instruct (Apache-2.0, multilingual) with a small Leukerbad facts file and a tool call `go_to(place)` → the robot vendor's navigation to pre-recorded waypoints → text-to-speech. All inference on-board; no internet.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm Omnicor support and target compute; run the LLM on a laptop behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint; record 4–5 waypoints with the robot vendor's tools.\n  - H6–24: wire speech in / out and the `go_to` tool; hand the model to Omnicor for on-device porting in parallel.\n  - H24–40: switch inference to the robot / OC Node; tune response latency; add four languages and a \"follow me\" pace suited to slow walkers.\n  - H40–48: rehearse; freeze; record a backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Judge asks in French where the thermal bath entrance is. (2) Robot answers and leads to the marked spot. (3) Teammate visibly pulls the Wi-Fi router plug; second judge asks in German; robot still answers and guides. (4) Show the latency / power readout and one slide on privacy.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** port not ready → keep the LLM on a laptop in a backpack or on the local LAN (still offline). Noisy hall → push-to-talk button or typed input. Navigation flaky → short straight route with two waypoints.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation moderate · technical depth in on-device multilingual dialogue · impact: tourism and elderly guests · Leukerbad relevance high (multilingual resort, patchy coverage).\n\n### Concept 2 — \"Last ten metres\" delivery hand-over\n\n- **Problem → value:** Delivery robots usually fail at the final step: taking the item off the shelf and handing it to a person. A mobile manipulator fetches a towel, pharmacy bag or room-service item and hands it over, with the policy running on-board.\n- **Hardware:** Galbot G1 or AGIBOT G1 / G2 (both listed as Omnicor customers on its site; scope ⚠️ unverified) or Galaxea R1 · OC Agent / OC Node for the on-board model.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera → object and person detection → the robot vendor's own grasp model (Galbot GraspVLA family, AGIBOT GO-1) or OpenVLA / pi0.5 → vendor arm control; base motion via vendor navigation between two stations; a local LLM parses the order (\"bring a towel to seat 3\"). Omnicor's job: make the chosen model run on the robot's computer at a usable rate.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: ask Omnicor and the robot vendor which model already runs on-board that robot; pick that one, do not start a new port if a working one exists.\n  - H6–24: scripted pick from a fixed shelf and hand-over at a fixed station; order parsing on laptop.\n  - H24–40: move order parsing on-device; add two or three item types; add safe hand-over (wait for the person's hand, release slowly).\n  - H40–48: run 20 trials, report success rate honestly; record backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Spoken order. (2) Robot drives to the shelf, picks the item. (3) Drives to the \"guest\", hands over. (4) Network unplugged, second order succeeds. (5) Slide: success rate and on-board latency.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** VLA unreliable on new objects → restrict to two known items or use the vendor's scripted grasp. No time to fine-tune → pretrained behaviour only. Robot access limited → simulate the base, show the arm only.\n- **Judging angles:** technical depth high (on-board VLA) · real-world impact for hotels and clinics · innovation depends on how robust the hand-over looks.\n\n### Concept 3 — Street and facility inspector with offline ticketing\n\n- **Problem → value:** A small municipality cannot patrol every path for ice, litter, overfull bins or blocked walkways. A quadruped does the round, classifies issues on-board and files tickets locally; it syncs when coverage returns.\n- **Hardware:** Unitree Go2 / B2 or Genisom M1 / L1 · on-board camera · OC Node or the robot's own computer running a compact vision-language model · optional SKYLANDX MetaCam scan for the map.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera frames → small detector for candidate issues → local vision-language model writes a one-line description and severity → ticket stored with pose from the robot's localisation → simple web dashboard on the LAN. Route following via the vendor SDK.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: define four issue classes; collect photos around the venue; confirm which vision-language model Omnicor can put on-device.\n  - H6–24: detector plus description pipeline on laptop; dashboard; patrol route.\n  - H24–40: on-device port; measure frames per second and battery impact with and without optimisation.\n  - H40–48: staged course with planted issues; rehearse.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Robot walks a short course with a planted \"ice patch\" mat, a litter pile and a blocked path. (2) Tickets appear on the dashboard with photo, text, location. (3) Show the same round with the network off, then sync. (4) One number: on-device rate versus the unoptimised baseline.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** vision-language model too slow on-board → detector only, text from templates. Outdoor snow or cold stops the robot → indoor mock street. No optimisation gain to show → present offline operation as the value.\n- **Judging angles:** Leukerbad relevance high (winter paths, small works department) · impact clear · technical depth in edge perception · innovation moderate.\n\n### Concept 4 — Town edge brain for a mixed robot fleet (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** A resort will own robots from several makers. Instead of each calling a foreign cloud, one local compute node runs the shared language and planning models and dispatches tasks: a quadruped carries, a mobile manipulator loads and hands over.\n- **Hardware:** OC Node as the local server (⚠️ unverified; fallback: any GPU machine) · one quadruped (Unitree Go2) · one wheeled dual-arm robot (Galbot G1 or AGIBOT G1) · optional Binabik AI or coprod software layer for cross-robot interfaces · optional XREAL glasses for an operator view.\n- **Architecture / stack:** request (voice or app) → local LLM agent on the node decomposes it into robot tasks → per-robot adapters call each vendor SDK → on-robot models handle perception and grasping → status back to a dashboard. Matches Omnicor's \"enterprise AI server\" plus \"embodied robots\" domains.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: fix one scenario (guest orders an item, manipulator loads it onto the quadruped, quadruped delivers); write the adapter interface; confirm node availability.\n  - H6–24: each robot performs its half alone, triggered by a fixed command.\n  - H24–40: LLM dispatcher on the node; hand-off between robots; failure handling (retry, ask human).\n  - H40–48: reduce scope to what works; rehearse the full chain five times.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Spoken request. (2) Dashboard shows the plan split across two robots. (3) Manipulator loads; quadruped delivers. (4) Internet cable pulled, LAN only, second request runs. (5) Closing slide: one local node, many robots, data stays in the valley.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** two vendors' SDKs in 48 h is a lot → drop to one robot plus a simulated second. Robot-to-robot hand-off fails → human places the item, keep the dispatch story. Node not available → laptop server.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation and technical depth highest · strong sovereignty and scalability story · execution risk highest.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\nEffort ratings are estimates; none of these integrations is documented publicly.\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| Omnicor AI + Galbot G1 | On-board grasp / VLA for delivery hand-over; possibly an existing relationship (site logo) | Medium, lower if a deployment already exists ⚠️ unverified |\n| Omnicor AI + AGIBOT G1 / G2 / X2 | Same, on wheeled dual-arm or humanoid; GO-1 as the policy | Medium ⚠️ unverified |\n| Omnicor AI + Unitree Go2 / B2 | Offline concierge or patrol inspector with a local LLM or vision-language model | Low to medium (compute box plus network link to the robot) |\n| Omnicor AI + D-Robotics RDK X5 / S100 | Target board for optimisation: show a model that did not fit now running on a small board | Medium; support for this silicon not confirmed |\n| Omnicor AI + Binabik AI | Agentic OS on top, optimised on-device models underneath | Medium to high (two young software stacks) |\n| Omnicor AI + SKYLANDX MetaCam | Fast 3D map of the venue for patrol or delivery routes while models run on-board | Low (map is prepared offline) |\n| Omnicor AI + XREAL glasses | Operator or supervisor view of tickets and robot status served from the local node | Low to medium |\n\n## Questions to ask the Omnicor AI engineers on site\n\n1. What did you physically bring: OC Node units (how many, which chip, power input, interfaces, mounting) or software and people only?\n2. Which robots here already run an Omnicor deployment, and with which models (GO-1, GraspVLA, OpenVLA, pi0.5)?\n3. How long does OC Agent need to port a new model to a given robot computer: hours or days? What do you need from us (weights format, sample inputs, accuracy target)?\n4. How do we call a deployed model: ROS 2 node, HTTP / gRPC, Python library? Is there a streaming interface for LLM tokens?\n5. What control rate do you reach for a VLA on-board, and what response time for an 8B-class LLM such as Apertus?\n6. Which local speech-to-text and vision-language models can you deploy alongside the LLM on one device?\n7. Can you give us before / after numbers (latency, memory, watts) that we may show in the final pitch?\n8. What was the L4 logistics-vehicle work: which sensors and models, and is any of it reusable for a small delivery robot?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Omnicor AI for T01 if** your idea's selling point is a service or delivery robot that keeps working offline (or keeps data private), you already have a robot from another vendor, and the Omnicor team confirms in the first hours that they can put your model on that robot's compute.\n- **Avoid if** you need a robot body, navigation or documented APIs from this vendor, if nobody from Omnicor can commit time to your team, or if your demo looks the same with a laptop or cloud model.\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [OpenVLA repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [pi0.5 base model in LeRobot (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n- [Apertus 8B Instruct model card (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (internal)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n","Omnicor-AI-T02-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T02\nchallenge_title: \"Resilient & Safe City\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, brief]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T02 Resilient & Safe City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — Not a robot and no public safety track record, but \"the model keeps running on the robot when the network is gone\" is exactly the resilience argument; usable only as the on-device inference layer of a multi-vendor build.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **OC Node** (in-house compute platform) + **OC Agent** (agentic deployment environment) + model optimisation service. All specs **not public**; availability on site ⚠️ unverified |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in this domain.** Only a website claim (2026, undated) that AgiBot, Galbot and \"sudo\" ship with OmniCor AI, and that it targets embodied-AI robots, L4 logistics vehicles and AI servers |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | \"Cut the cable\" patrol: a quadruped or sensor mast runs hazard detection + a local LLM incident report fully on-board; unplug Wi-Fi live and show it still works |\n| Biggest risk | Nothing to prepare against: no SDK, docs, GitHub or specs. If the team brings no hardware or a port takes days, you have no Omnicor component left |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Unitree Go2 / B2 or AGIBOT D1 (patrol body) · SKYLANDX MetaCam (3D map) · D-Robotics RDK (fallback edge board) · AGIBOT / Galbot (listed as Omnicor customers) · XREAL (responder display) |\n\n## Why\n- **Offline is the T02 story.** Avalanche, storm or tunnel = no cloud. Inference on the device is the only inference left.\n- **Model list fits.** The site names open LLMs (Apertus, Mistral, Qwen, DeepSeek) and robot VLAs (OpenVLA, pi0.5) it deploys — enough for local reasoning and reporting in German / French.\n- **Customer overlap on site.** AgiBot and Galbot appear on the Omnicor site; a working deployment may already be in the hall (ask).\n- **Measurable judging angle:** latency, watts, \"works with the router off\".\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams needing a robot, sensors, mapping or navigation from one vendor — Omnicor supplies none.\n- Teams wanting a documented, self-serve SDK tonight — none is public.\n- Projects where a cloud API is acceptable.\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T02-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/T02-0-Track-Overview.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI on LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/omnicor-ai/) (via profile research; not readable without login)\n","Omnicor-AI-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, products]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T02 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T02-3-Playbook.md)\n\n> **Read this first.** OmniCor AI publishes a one-page website with headings only. There are **no specs, prices, docs, SDK or case studies** for any product, and nothing specific to safety, inspection or emergency response. Every table below therefore says \"not public\" where a number would normally be. Treat this page as a list of what to verify at the booth, not as a datasheet.\n> Research note: the track-specific web search could not be run (search budget exhausted); findings rest on the official site, re-read on 2026-11-14, plus the company profile.\n\n**Role in T02 in one line:** the on-device inference layer — it makes a detection model, a local LLM or a VLA run on the robot or sensor itself, so the system keeps working when the network does not.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### OC Node\n\n- **Role in T02:** the compute box that rides on a patrol robot or sits at a sensor mast and runs perception + language models with no backhaul. This is the most demo-able Omnicor item for a \"resilient\" story.\n- **What the site says:** a compute platform designed in-house. Nothing more.\n\n| Track-relevant spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Chip / accelerator | Not public (ecosystem logos on site: Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD — which one is inside OC Node is not stated) |\n| Compute (TOPS / memory) | Not public |\n| Power draw / supply voltage | Not public — decisive for battery-powered patrol robots |\n| Operating temperature, IP rating, shock / vibration | Not public — decisive for outdoor alpine use in November |\n| Interfaces (Ethernet, USB, CAN, camera inputs) | Not public |\n| Weight / mounting | Not public |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified (listed on the site; whether it ships, and whether units are in Leukerbad, is not confirmed). Price not public.\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Assume nothing: ask whether you get a shell (Linux?), containers, a ROS 2 node, or only a model endpoint.\n\n### OC Agent\n\n- **Role in T02:** the tooling that fits your model to a target device. In a 48-hour build its value is speed: getting a detector, VLM or local LLM running on the robot's own computer (or an OC Node) without you hand-tuning quantisation and runtimes.\n- **What the site says:** an agentic deployment environment. The contact form asks for \"your model and your target hardware\", which reads as a service-led workflow rather than a self-serve download.\n\n| Track-relevant spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Supported target hardware | Not public (Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD shown as ecosystem) |\n| Supported model types | Site lists LLMs (Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek) and VLAs (OpenVLA, pi0.5 via LeRobot), \"and more\". Classic vision detectors are not mentioned ⚠️ unverified |\n| Time to port a model | Not public — the key question for a hackathon |\n| Output (runtime, API, container) | Not public |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified. Price not public. No public sign-up, docs or repository found.\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Likely operated by, or together with, the Omnicor engineers on site (inference).\n\n### Model optimisation\n\n- **Role in T02:** shrinking a model to fit a power, latency and memory budget. For safety systems this buys longer battery patrols and faster reaction (detect → alert) on small hardware.\n- **Specs:** no method, benchmark or speed-up figure is published.\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified service; price not public.\n- **Developer interface:** you hand over a model; what comes back (format, accuracy report) is not public. Ask for before / after numbers you can show to judges.\n\n### European delivery\n\n- **Role in T02:** not a build component. It is a pitch argument: public-safety and critical-infrastructure buyers care about where hardware is designed and built, and the site states design and manufacturing in Europe.\n- **Specs / status / price:** not public; ⚠️ unverified (no facility, location or certification named).\n\n## What Omnicor AI already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing found that is specific to T02.** No emergency-response, inspection, security-patrol, hazard-monitoring or digital-twin deployment, pilot, demo or partner has been published.\n\nAdjacent facts from the official site (2026, undated):\n\n| Item | Relevance to T02 | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Deployment domain \"embodied AI robotics\": perception and control that must run on-board a moving platform | Same constraint as a patrol or rescue robot without connectivity | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| Deployment domain \"L4 autonomous logistics vehicles\": continuous multi-sensor inference under automotive thermal and reliability limits | Closest hint at ruggedised, always-on operation; no product or customer named | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| Customers shown: AgiBot, Galbot, \"sudo\" | AgiBot and Galbot robots are on site; scope of the work is not public; \"sudo\" ⚠️ unverified | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| Partner logo HUIXI; ecosystem logos Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD | Suggests which silicon they can target; HUIXI identity ⚠️ unverified | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced:** nothing. No roadmap, launch date or safety-related plan is public.\n- **Inference (not a company statement):** the automotive-style wording (thermal and reliability constraints) and the in-house compute node point towards ruggedised edge compute, which would suit outdoor safety robots later. Do not present this as a plan.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\nOmnicor publishes **no code, models or datasets** of its own. What shortcuts a 48-hour build are the third-party open models its site says it deploys:\n\n| Asset | Useful for in T02 | Verified facts | Licence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Apertus** (Swiss AI Initiative) | Local incident reports and voice / text Q&A for responders, offline, multilingual | **8B** and **70B** sizes; **1,811** natively supported languages; context up to **65,536** tokens | Apache-2.0 |\n| **OpenVLA** | On-board manipulation policy (e.g. operate a switch or valve) on a mobile manipulator | `openvla-7b` checkpoint, about **7.5 B** parameters; LoRA fine-tuning needs roughly **27 GB+** GPU memory per the repo | Code MIT; weights carry Llama-2 community-licence terms |\n| **pi0.5 in LeRobot** | Same role as OpenVLA with a maintained training / rollout toolchain | `pip install 'lerobot[pi]'`, `policy.type=pi05`; base checkpoints `lerobot/pi05_base`, `lerobot/pi05_libero_base`; example fine-tune sized for one **80 GB** GPU | Apache-2.0 per LeRobot docs; `lerobot/pi05_base` weights card tagged `gemma` |\n| Mistral AI, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenEuroLLM | Alternative local LLMs / VLMs | Named on the Omnicor site only; sizes and variants supported by Omnicor not public | Varies by model |\n\nPractical notes:\n\n- **Whether Omnicor has these models ready-to-run on hardware in the hall is ⚠️ unverified** — the site lists them as \"models we deploy\" without device, speed or accuracy figures.\n- VLA fine-tuning needs data and a large GPU; inside 48 hours, treat VLAs as \"use a pre-trained or vendor-provided policy\" unless the team brings compute.\n- For hazard detection (smoke, person down, blocked exit) a small vision model or a compact VLM is the realistic choice; ask whether OC Agent handles such models.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No robot, sensor, mapping, navigation or teleoperation product.** You must bring the body and the perception stack from another vendor.\n- **No public developer access.** No SDK, API reference, repo, simulator or sample; you cannot prepare before talking to the team.\n- **No environmental data** for OC Node (temperature, IP, shock) — an outdoor alpine claim cannot be backed with numbers.\n- **No proof points in safety or inspection**, and no named deployments at all beyond three customer logos.\n- **Service dependency.** If porting needs Omnicor engineers and they are shared across many teams, your critical path sits in someone else's queue.\n- **Very small company (2–10 people per LinkedIn, ⚠️ unverified):** limited on-site support capacity is likely (inference).\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/) (single page; sections \"Where our deployments run\", \"What we build\", \"Models we deploy\", customers, partners)\n- [Omnicor AI on LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/omnicor-ai/) (company size; via profile research, page not readable without login)\n- [OpenVLA GitHub repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card (Hugging Face, swiss-ai)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [LeRobot documentation: pi0.5 policy](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05)\n- [pi0.5 base model card in LeRobot (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n","Omnicor-AI-T02-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, playbook]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T02 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n> **Ground rule for every concept below:** Omnicor AI is the on-device inference layer, never the whole system. Nothing about OC Node or OC Agent is publicly specified, so each concept names a **Plan B compute** (robot's own computer, a laptop, or a D-Robotics RDK board) that keeps the project alive if the Omnicor part is not available. Spend the first hour at their booth before committing.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — \"Cut the cable\" hazard sentinel (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Fixed cameras at a tunnel portal, cable-car station or avalanche-prone road stop being useful the moment the uplink fails. A sentinel that detects, reasons and alerts locally keeps protecting during the outage.\n- **Hardware:** OC Node (if available) + USB / IP camera on a tripod. Plan B compute: laptop or D-Robotics RDK. No robot required.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera → small detector or compact VLM (person down, smoke, blocked exit, debris on road) → rule layer for alarm thresholds → local LLM (Apertus 8B or a Qwen / Mistral variant) turns events into a short incident report in German / French / English → local dashboard + siren / light over GPIO or LAN. All compute on the node; network used only to sync when it returns.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm what Omnicor can run tonight; pick 3 hazard classes; get camera → detector running on Plan B.\n  - H6–24: port detector + LLM to OC Node with the Omnicor team; build event log and report prompt.\n  - H24–40: add store-and-forward sync, measure latency and power, stage the scenes.\n  - H40–48: rehearse; record a backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) show live dashboard; (2) pull the Ethernet cable on stage; (3) a teammate \"collapses\" or a smoke machine / cardboard \"rockfall\" enters the scene; (4) alarm fires and a three-language report appears in seconds; (5) plug the cable back, the log syncs; (6) show latency and watts on one slide.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** OC Node absent → run on Plan B and present Omnicor as the path to production. False alarms → restrict to 2 classes with staged scenes.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation modest; technical depth = measured offline latency and power; impact = works in outages; Leukerbad = road and gallery closures, cable-car stations, thermal-bath plant rooms.\n\n### Concept 2 — Offline patrol dog for infrastructure rounds\n\n- **Problem → value:** After a storm or avalanche warning, someone must check galleries, plant rooms and paths. A quadruped that inspects and reports without connectivity removes people from the hazard and still works in a tunnel.\n- **Hardware:** quadruped from another vendor (Unitree Go2 / B2, AGIBOT D1, Genisom M1 / L1 or Magiclab MagicDog) + OC Node as payload, or Omnicor-optimised models on the robot's own computer. Optional SKYLANDX MetaCam for a prior 3D map.\n- **Architecture / stack:** robot's stock locomotion and waypoint navigation (vendor SDK / ROS 2) → on-board camera frames → VLM checklist per waypoint (\"is the door closed?\", \"is there water on the floor?\", \"read the gauge\") → local LLM compiles a round report → stored on the robot, offloaded when back in range. Omnicor layer = making the VLM + LLM fit the robot's power and memory budget.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: secure a robot slot; check payload power / mounting for OC Node; teleoperate a route.\n  - H6–24: waypoint patrol working; VLM checklist on Plan B compute; start port to OC Node.\n  - H24–40: integrate on-board, run with Wi-Fi off, tune prompts on 4–5 inspection points, add anomaly photo log.\n  - H40–48: rehearse full round; fallback video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) show the robot's router being switched off; (2) dog walks three checkpoints; (3) one has a staged fault (open cabinet, \"leak\", obstacle); (4) dog returns, report with photos appears on a tablet; (5) one slide: on-board inference time per checkpoint, battery impact.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** payload mounting or power not possible → run models on the robot's built-in computer or a backpack laptop. Navigation unreliable → teleoperate the walk, keep inspection autonomous. VLM too slow → inspect only when stopped.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation = fully offline semantic inspection; depth = navigation + on-board VLM; impact = fewer people in hazard zones; Leukerbad = steep, snowy paths, avalanche galleries on the access road, spa technical rooms.\n\n### Concept 3 — Responder copilot: local map + local language model\n\n- **Problem → value:** First responders entering a damaged or smoke-filled building need \"what is where\" hands-free, and radio / mobile coverage is often the first thing lost. A wearable or carried unit that answers questions from a local 3D scan and live detections needs no network.\n- **Hardware:** OC Node as a belt / backpack compute unit + SKYLANDX MetaCam (scan) + XREAL glasses (display). Plan B compute: laptop in a backpack.\n- **Architecture / stack:** MetaCam scan → point cloud / floor plan with tagged objects (exits, extinguishers, hazards, detected persons) → structured scene memory → local LLM answers voice / text queries (\"nearest exit?\", \"where was the last person seen?\") → overlay or text on XREAL. Speech-to-text and LLM both on-device; multilingual output via Apertus.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: scan one corridor + room; export format agreed; LLM answering from a hand-written scene file.\n  - H6–24: automatic tagging from detections; query interface; XREAL text view.\n  - H24–40: port models to OC Node; latency tuning; offline test walk.\n  - H40–48: rehearse, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) show the pre-scan; (2) responder with glasses enters, phone in flight mode; (3) asks two questions aloud in German or French; (4) answers and direction appear; (5) a new \"casualty\" is detected and added to the map; (6) incident summary exported.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** three vendors to integrate → drop XREAL and use a phone / tablet screen. Live map update too hard → static pre-scan plus live detections list. Speech recognition unreliable in noise → push-to-talk or text.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation = language interface over a local digital twin; depth = SLAM + LLM grounding on edge compute; impact = responder safety; Leukerbad = hotels, clinics and bath complexes with many elderly guests to evacuate.\n\n### Concept 4 — Comms-loss handover: teleoperated manipulator that finishes the job alone (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Robots in hazardous zones are teleoperated, and the link is the weak point. When it drops mid-task (closing a valve, flipping a breaker, clearing a small obstacle), an on-board policy completes or safely aborts the action instead of freezing.\n- **Hardware:** wheeled dual-arm manipulator from an Omnicor-listed customer (AGIBOT G1 / G2 or Galbot G1) + on-board VLA deployed with Omnicor (OpenVLA or pi0.5), on OC Node or the robot's computer.\n- **Architecture / stack:** operator teleop over Wi-Fi → link-quality monitor → on loss, switch to on-board VLA with a language goal (\"close the red valve\") or to a safe-retreat behaviour → on reconnect, hand back with a status report from a local LLM. Use the vendor's own pre-trained model where possible (AGIBOT GO-1, Galbot GraspVLA family) rather than training from scratch.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm with Omnicor and the robot vendor that a VLA already runs on-board; if not, **stop and pick Concept 2**.\n  - H6–24: teleop baseline for one task; collect demonstrations if fine-tuning is feasible; link monitor.\n  - H24–40: autonomous completion of one task on a mock panel; handover logic; safe-abort path.\n  - H40–48: rehearse 10 runs, report success rate honestly; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) operator starts turning a valve by teleop; (2) teammate kills the Wi-Fi; (3) status light changes, robot finishes the turn or retreats; (4) Wi-Fi back, operator receives a spoken / text summary; (5) show success count from rehearsals.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no ready on-board VLA → scripted motion primitive as the \"autonomous\" finisher, VLA shown as roadmap. Fine-tuning compute missing (pi0.5 examples assume a large GPU) → use a vendor pre-trained skill. Safety → low speed, soft mock-up, e-stop in hand.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation high (graceful autonomy under comms loss); depth high (teleop + VLA + on-device inference); impact = real hazardous-environment need; Leukerbad = thermal-water plant, tunnels and cable-car machinery where radio coverage is poor.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| Omnicor + Unitree Go2 / B2 | Offline patrol and inspection (Concept 2) | Medium — payload power / mounting and model port are the unknowns |\n| Omnicor + AGIBOT (D1, G1 / G2, GO-1) | Patrol or manipulation on a robot whose maker is listed as an Omnicor customer | Medium — lower if a deployment already exists (⚠️ unverified) |\n| Omnicor + Galbot G1 | On-board VLA manipulation under comms loss (Concept 4) | High — depends on an existing on-board model |\n| Omnicor + SKYLANDX MetaCam | Local 3D map as memory for an offline LLM (Concept 3) | Medium — data export format to settle early |\n| Omnicor + XREAL | Hands-free responder display of local inference results | Medium — keep to text overlay |\n| Omnicor + D-Robotics RDK | Low-cost target board and Plan B compute; \"any device\" story with two targets | Low to medium — whether OC Agent supports this board is ⚠️ unverified |\n| Omnicor + Genisom / Magiclab quadrupeds | Alternative patrol bodies if Unitree slots run out | Medium |\n| Omnicor + Binabik AI | Agent OS on top, optimised models underneath | High — two young software stacks, interfaces not public |\n\n## Questions to ask the Omnicor AI engineers on site\n\n1. What did you physically bring: OC Node units (how many?), and what is the chip, memory, power draw and supply voltage?\n2. Can OC Node run on a quadruped's battery rail, and is there any temperature / IP rating for outdoor use?\n3. Which robots in this hall already run an Omnicor deployment (AgiBot? Galbot?), with which model, at what inference rate?\n4. How long does OC Agent need to port a new model — a small detector, a compact VLM, an 8B LLM? Minutes, hours or days?\n5. What do we get back: a ROS 2 node, an HTTP / gRPC endpoint, a container, or a binary? Can we log in to the device?\n6. Does OC Agent handle classic vision models and speech-to-text, or only LLMs and VLAs?\n7. Can Apertus (or Qwen / Mistral) run fully offline on your hardware with usable speed in German and French? Tokens per second?\n8. Can you give us before / after numbers (latency, memory, watts, accuracy) that we may show to the judges?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Omnicor AI for T02 if** your core claim is \"it still works with no network\", you already have a robot or sensor from another vendor, and the Omnicor team confirms in the first hour that your model can run on hardware they have here.\n- **Avoid if** you need a complete platform, documented APIs tonight, or your demo does not visibly benefit from on-device inference; choose a robot vendor first and treat Omnicor as optional.\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (this repository)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n- [OpenVLA GitHub repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card (Hugging Face, swiss-ai)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [LeRobot documentation: pi0.5 policy](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05)\n","Omnicor-AI-T03-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T03\nchallenge_title: \"Sustainable City\"\nfit: 2\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, brief]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T03 Sustainable City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 2/5** — Supporting role only: an edge-AI deployment team (no robots, no public specs or SDK) that can make another vendor's robot run its model on-board at lower power; useful as the \"low-power edge AI\" layer of a T03 project, not as its core.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **Model optimisation** service plus **OC Node** compute box (if units are on site — ⚠️ unverified); specs not public |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in this domain.** Site (2026, undated) only names use cases: on-board robot inference inside a power budget, and server inference where throughput per watt sets operating cost. No case study, no numbers |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Camera + small arm waste-sorting station running a vision model fully on-device, with a live **joules-per-sorted-item** meter comparing baseline vs optimised model |\n| Biggest risk | Nothing self-serve: no docs, SDK or published hardware. If the team cannot port your model within hours, you have no Omnicor component in the demo |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Galbot and AGIBOT (both shown as customers on Omnicor's site), Dobot (sorting arm), D-Robotics (RDK boards as comparison or fallback compute), Unitree Go2 (patrol / monitoring) |\n\n## Why\n- T03 explicitly rewards lower emissions and smarter resources; **energy per inference** is the one sustainability lever Omnicor can move, and it is measurable with a wall-plug meter.\n- On-device inference removes the cloud round trip: works with patchy alpine connectivity and gives a clean \"no data-centre\" story.\n- The site lists **OpenVLA** and **pi0.5 (LeRobot)** among deployed models, so a manipulation policy for sorting or litter picking is in their stated scope.\n- Customer logos for **Galbot** and **AGIBOT** suggest an existing integration path to robots that are physically on site (scope not public).\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams that need a robot, sensors or a documented API from a single vendor — Omnicor provides none publicly.\n- Projects whose value is physical (waste handling, EV charging) and whose model already runs on a laptop: the extra integration buys little.\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T03-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T03-Sustainable-City/T03-0-Track-Overview.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n","Omnicor-AI-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, products]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T03 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T03-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** Omnicor AI (written \"OmniCor AI\" on its site) publishes a single one-page website with headings only. There are **no specs, prices, docs, SDK or case studies**. Every capability below is \"ask on site\". Nothing on the site addresses sustainability, waste, energy management or environmental monitoring directly; the T03 link is **lower energy per inference** and **cloud-free operation**.\n\nResearch note: general web search was unavailable for this pass; findings rest on the official site (re-read 2026-11-14) and the official pages of the open-source projects listed under Sources.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### Model optimisation\n\n- **Role in T03:** the only item that maps onto a sustainability metric. Shrinking or accelerating a model so it fits a power, latency and memory budget means fewer joules per decision, smaller batteries or longer runtime per charge.\n- **Status:** ⚠️ unverified (listed on site, no description) · **Price:** not public · **Availability:** by contact (\"tell us about your model and your target hardware\").\n\n| Track-relevant spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Techniques used (quantisation, pruning, distillation, compilation) | Not public |\n| Energy or speed gain achieved | Not public |\n| Models named on site | Apertus, Mistral AI, Open Euro LLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenVLA, pi0.5 (LeRobot), \"and others\" |\n| Target silicon (ecosystem logos) | Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD |\n| Turnaround for a new model | Not public |\n\n- **Developer interface:** none published. Service-style engagement: you hand over a trained model and a target device; they return a deployment. What you can tune yourself is unknown.\n\n### OC Node\n\n- **Role in T03:** a compute box to bolt onto a robot or sorting station so inference runs locally at low power instead of on a GPU workstation or in the cloud.\n- **Status:** ⚠️ unverified (site says \"compute platform designed in-house\"; no photo, no datasheet) · **Price:** not public · **Availability:** not public; on-site presence unconfirmed.\n\n| Track-relevant spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Chip / accelerator | Not public |\n| Power draw (idle / load) | Not public |\n| Memory, I/O (USB, Ethernet, CAN), supply voltage | Not public |\n| Weight, size, IP rating, operating temperature | Not public |\n\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Ask whether it runs standard Linux with ROS 2 and whether you get shell access.\n\n### OC Agent\n\n- **Role in T03:** described only as an \"agentic deployment environment\". Plausible reading (inference): an automated workflow that fits a model to a target chip. For T03 it matters only as the thing that makes porting fast enough for 48 hours.\n- **Status:** ⚠️ unverified · **Price:** not public · **Availability:** not public; no sign-up, CLI or package found.\n\n| Track-relevant spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Inputs / outputs, supported runtimes | Not public |\n| Self-serve or operated by Omnicor staff | Not public |\n| Reports energy or latency per model variant | Not public |\n\n- **Developer interface:** not public.\n\n### European delivery\n\n- **Role in T03:** marginal. \"Designed and built in Europe\" could support a short-supply-chain or data-sovereignty argument in a pitch, but there is nothing to build with. No location, factory or volume is public. Status ⚠️ unverified.\n\n## What Omnicor AI already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing specific to sustainable-city applications is public.** No deployment, pilot, demo, partnership or application page for waste, recycling, energy, EV charging, environmental monitoring or urban farming was found.\n\nClosest adjacent statements, all from the official site (2026, undated, no detail):\n\n| Statement | T03 relevance | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Robot deployments must run on-board within the moving platform's power budget | Energy-efficient mobile robots | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| Enterprise AI servers: throughput per watt drives operating cost | Efficiency of inference infrastructure | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| L4 autonomous logistics vehicles: continuous multi-sensor inference under thermal limits | Resource-efficient logistics (indirect) | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| Customer logos: AgiBot, Galbot, \"sudo\" | Possible existing path onto robots that are on site; scope not public | [omnicorai.io](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced:** nothing. No roadmap, news or release notes exist publicly.\n- **Inference only:** the site's positioning (European and open models, Qualcomm / NVIDIA / AMD silicon, own compute node, built in Europe) points to a sovereign, efficient edge-inference offering. Energy efficiency is a selling point in that pitch, not a product line. Do not expect sustainability-specific tooling.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\nOmnicor publishes no code. These third-party open resources are what you would actually build with; the first four are models the Omnicor site names.\n\n| Resource | What it gives you for T03 | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| [OpenVLA](https://github.com/openvla/openvla) | Open vision-language-action policy for pick / sort tasks | 7B-class model; code MIT, weights inherit Llama-2 terms. LoRA fine-tune needs a GPU with at least about **27 GB**; ships a REST server script (`deploy.py`) usable as off-board fallback |\n| [LeRobot](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot) (pi0.5, SmolVLA, ACT) | Record demos, train and run policies with `lerobot-record` / `lerobot-train` | Apache-2.0. ACT or SmolVLA are the realistic choices to train inside 48 h |\n| [openpi](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi) | Reference code and checkpoints for the pi0 family | Heavier; use only if a checkpoint already fits your robot |\n| [Apertus 8B](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509) | Swiss open LLM for an offline, multilingual operator or resident interface | Apache-2.0; quantised community builds exist for local runtimes |\n| [Ultralytics YOLO](https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics) | Small detectors (smallest YOLO26 model about **2.4M** parameters) with ONNX / TensorRT export | AGPL-3.0; a strong low-power baseline for waste or litter detection |\n| [TACO](https://github.com/pedropro/TACO) and [TrashNet](https://github.com/garythung/trashnet) | Open litter and waste-category image datasets | Check licences and class lists before training |\n| [CodeCarbon](https://github.com/mlco2/codecarbon) | Estimates energy and CO2 of compute from CPU / GPU / RAM use | MIT. Pair with a wall-plug meter for a number judges trust |\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No robot, sensor or actuator.** Every T03 build needs another vendor's hardware.\n- **No public numbers.** No watts, tokens per second, control rate or energy savings to quote; you must measure on site.\n- **No self-serve tooling.** Without Omnicor staff time, there is no Omnicor contribution.\n- **Indirect impact.** Inference is usually a small share of a robot's energy use next to motors; an honest pitch must say so or pick a task where compute dominates (static sorting station, always-on monitoring).\n- **Unknown on-site kit.** OC Node presence, quantity and supported robots are unconfirmed.\n- **Licensing detail.** OpenVLA weights and AGPL detectors carry conditions; fine for a hackathon, relevant afterwards.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [OpenVLA (GitHub)](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [LeRobot (GitHub)](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot)\n- [openpi (GitHub)](https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi)\n- [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [Ultralytics YOLO (GitHub)](https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics)\n- [TACO dataset (GitHub)](https://github.com/pedropro/TACO)\n- [TrashNet dataset (GitHub)](https://github.com/garythung/trashnet)\n- [CodeCarbon (GitHub)](https://github.com/mlco2/codecarbon)\n","Omnicor-AI-T03-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, playbook]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T03 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Fit 2/5 — supporting role.** Omnicor AI has no robot and no public SDK, specs or docs. Every concept below uses another vendor's hardware and treats Omnicor as the on-device inference layer. **Each concept is designed to still work without Omnicor** (fallback compute named), so a \"no\" from their engineers does not sink the project. All Omnicor capabilities are ⚠️ unverified until confirmed on site.\n\n**The one idea that makes Omnicor relevant to T03:** measure and cut **energy per decision** (joules per sorted item, per patrol, per answer) and show the number live.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Joule-metered waste-sorting station (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Mixed waste in hotels and clinics lowers recycling quality. A small sorting cell that classifies and separates items, running entirely on a low-power edge box, shows automation that does not add a data-centre footprint.\n- **Hardware:** fixed RGB camera, one small arm (Dobot Magician or CR cobot; alternatives ROKAE xMate, Flexiv Rizon), bins, wall-plug power meter. Compute: **OC Node** if available; otherwise a D-Robotics RDK board, a Jetson-class board or a laptop.\n- **Architecture / stack:** perception = small detector (YOLO-class, fine-tuned on TACO / TrashNet plus 100–200 photos of local items) → planning = class-to-bin lookup and fixed pick poses → control = the arm vendor's own SDK. Omnicor's part: optimise the detector for the target box and report latency and watts. Energy logging: wall-plug meter plus CodeCarbon or the board's own power readout.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm arm, camera and compute; collect local item photos; record baseline power of the unoptimised model.\n  - H6–24: train the detector; hand-eye calibration; first pick-and-drop cycle; give the model to Omnicor for porting.\n  - H24–40: swap in the optimised model; build the dashboard (items sorted, accuracy, joules per item, baseline vs optimised).\n  - H40–48: tune reliability on 5–6 item types; rehearse; record a backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) the problem in one sentence, with a real bag of hotel waste. (0:30) judges drop items; the arm sorts them. (1:30) switch the dashboard between baseline and optimised model: same accuracy, fewer joules. (2:15) unplug the network cable — it keeps working. (2:40) scale-up arithmetic for one hotel, stated as an estimate.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** Omnicor cannot port in time → export the model to ONNX / TensorRT yourself and keep the energy comparison. Grasping fails on odd shapes → restrict to rigid items or use a suction tool. Savings look small → report them honestly and lead with offline operation.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — modest; technical depth — a measured energy claim, which is rare at hackathons; impact — recycling quality; Leukerbad — hotels, spas and clinics produce sorted-waste streams daily.\n\n### Concept 2 — Offline energy-waste patrol for spa and hotel buildings\n\n- **Problem → value:** Thermal resorts lose heat through tilted windows, propped doors and lights left on in unused rooms. A robot on a night round spots these and logs them for staff, with all inference on-board.\n- **Hardware:** Unitree Go2 (or another quadruped / wheeled base on site) with its RGB camera; optional thermal camera only if one is available (not confirmed). Compute: OC Node or the robot's own computer.\n- **Architecture / stack:** perception = detector for open window, open door, light on, radiator → planning = waypoint patrol with the robot vendor's navigation; findings list with location → interface = a local LLM (Apertus or Qwen, quantised) turns the log into a short staff report in German / French / English. Omnicor's part: run detector and LLM together inside the robot's power budget.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: get permission for a corridor; teleoperate a round and record video; define 4 detection classes.\n  - H6–24: label and train; waypoint patrol working; LLM running locally on a laptop.\n  - H24–40: move both models on-board; findings map; report generation.\n  - H40–48: full rounds, battery-per-round measurement, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) one photo of a tilted window above a radiator. (0:20) robot walks a short loop past staged faults. (1:30) the findings list appears; ask the LLM \"what should the night porter fix first?\" (2:15) show compute watts on-board vs a GPU laptop. (2:40) estimated savings, clearly labelled as estimate.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** navigation takes too long → teleoperate the round and keep detection autonomous. Detector unreliable → reduce to two classes (window, light). On-board port fails → run on a laptop carried alongside and say so.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — monitoring instead of manipulation; depth — two models sharing one edge budget; impact — heating is a major cost for thermal resorts; Leukerbad — very direct.\n\n### Concept 3 — On-board VLA litter and recyclables collector\n\n- **Problem → value:** Litter on promenades and around thermal baths is collected by hand. A mobile manipulator that picks and pre-sorts recyclables, with the policy running on the robot, removes both the tether and the cloud dependency.\n- **Hardware:** Galbot G1 or AGIBOT G1 / G2 (both vendors appear as Omnicor customers on its site, scope not public); alternative Galaxea R1. Compute: on-board, via Omnicor deployment or OC Node.\n- **Architecture / stack:** perception + control = a VLA or imitation policy (ACT / SmolVLA trained with LeRobot on 50–100 teleoperated demos; or the vendor's own model such as GraspVLA or GO-1) → planning = simple state machine (find, approach, pick, drop in the right bin) → Omnicor's part: fit the policy on-board and hold a usable control rate. Compare energy and latency against the same policy served from a GPU workstation (OpenVLA ships a REST server script that suits this baseline).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm with Omnicor **which robot and which model already run together**; choose the policy accordingly; set up teleoperation.\n  - H6–24: record demos for 3 object types (can, PET bottle, paper cup); train; first closed-loop picks off-board.\n  - H24–40: on-board deployment; state machine; energy logging.\n  - H40–48: success-rate runs, video, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) scatter three item types. (0:20) robot picks and bins them, untethered. (1:40) side-by-side: off-board GPU vs on-board, watts and control rate. (2:20) success rate over N trials, read out honestly. (2:40) what a season of promenade cleaning would need.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** policy training does not converge → scripted grasp from a detector, keep the on-board story. Robot access limited → move the task to a fixed arm (becomes Concept 1). On-board rate too low → smaller policy, or state the gap as the finding.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — medium; depth — high (learned policy, on-board deployment, measured trade-off); impact — labour and litter; Leukerbad — tourist promenades, snow-free season.\n\n### Concept 4 — Energy-aware model switching (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Robots run their largest model all the time. Switching between small, medium and large model variants according to battery level and task difficulty extends runtime per charge and cuts charging cycles.\n- **Hardware:** any battery robot from Concepts 2 or 3; OC Node or on-board computer.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Omnicor's tooling produces **several variants** of one model for the same chip (if OC Agent can do this — ask) → a supervisor reads battery state and detector confidence and selects the variant → log energy per task for fixed-large vs adaptive.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm variant generation is possible; define the task and the switching rule.\n  - H6–24: get two variants running; build the supervisor.\n  - H24–40: paired runs, fixed vs adaptive; dashboard.\n  - H40–48: polish and pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) the claim. (0:30) robot performs the task while the dashboard shows the active variant changing. (1:45) bar chart: tasks per charge, fixed vs adaptive. (2:30) how this generalises to a fleet.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** only one variant available → switch between two different open models. Gains masked by motor energy → use a compute-dominated task (static perception) and say so.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — highest of the four; depth — systems-level; impact — indirect but fleet-wide; Leukerbad — cold reduces battery capacity, so runtime matters in alpine winter.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| Omnicor + Dobot (Magician / CR) | Concept 1 sorting cell with on-device vision | Low — arm SDK is independent of the inference box |\n| Omnicor + D-Robotics RDK | Side-by-side edge boards, or RDK as fallback compute | Low to medium |\n| Omnicor + Unitree Go2 | Concept 2 patrol with on-board detection and local LLM | Medium — power and mounting for an extra box |\n| Omnicor + Galbot G1 | Concept 3; customer logo suggests prior integration (scope not public) | Medium to high — depends on what already runs |\n| Omnicor + AGIBOT G1 / G2 | Concept 3 with GO-1 or a LeRobot policy | Medium to high — same caveat |\n| Omnicor + SKYLANDX MetaCam | 3D scan of a building as the map for patrol findings | Medium — offline map only, not live SLAM on the robot |\n| Omnicor + XREAL glasses | Staff see energy-waste findings in AR | Medium — adds a second interface to build |\n\n## Questions to ask the Omnicor AI engineers on site\n\n1. What hardware did you bring — OC Node units? How many, which chip, what power draw, which interfaces and supply voltage?\n2. Which robots here already run an Omnicor deployment, and with which model (Galbot? AGIBOT?)?\n3. If we give you a fine-tuned detector or an ACT / SmolVLA policy tonight, when do we get it back running on-device — hours or days?\n4. Can we use OC Agent ourselves, or do you operate it? Can it output several size variants of one model?\n5. Do you have measured before / after numbers (watts, latency, accuracy) for any model we could reproduce live?\n6. How do we call the deployed model — ROS 2 node, HTTP / gRPC, Python binding?\n7. Can the box report its own power draw, or do we need an external meter?\n8. Can a local LLM (Apertus, Mistral, Qwen) and a vision model run at the same time on one unit?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Omnicor AI for T03 if** the engineers confirm at kickoff that they have hardware on site and can port your model within a day, and your pitch is built around a **measured** low-power, cloud-free result on another vendor's robot or arm.\n- **Avoid if** you need a single-vendor platform with documentation, your model already runs comfortably on a laptop and energy is not your story, or you get no clear commitment on turnaround in the first hours.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [OpenVLA (GitHub)](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [LeRobot (GitHub)](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot)\n- [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [Ultralytics YOLO (GitHub)](https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics)\n- [TACO dataset (GitHub)](https://github.com/pedropro/TACO)\n- [TrashNet dataset (GitHub)](https://github.com/garythung/trashnet)\n- [CodeCarbon (GitHub)](https://github.com/mlco2/codecarbon)\n","Omnicor-AI-T04-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T04\nchallenge_title: \"Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, brief]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — Not a care robot and no care track record, but a credible **privacy layer**: language and vision models running on the device, so no patient audio or video leaves the room. Needs another vendor's robot.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **OC Agent** (agentic deployment environment) plus **OC Node** (in-house compute box) — both ⚠️ unverified: no specs, SDK or availability are public. Use them under a robot from another vendor. |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in care or rehab.** Only general evidence: website (2026, undated) names AgiBot and Galbot as teams shipping with OmniCor AI and lists Apertus, Mistral, Qwen, OpenVLA and pi0.5 as models it deploys. |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Offline, multilingual (DE / FR / EN) voice companion and reminder assistant running a local LLM on an edge box next to a robot — network cable unplugged on stage. |\n| Biggest risk | Nothing is self-serve. If the team brings no OC Node or cannot port a model within hours, you are left with a generic open-source edge stack and no OmniCor differentiator. |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | **AgiBot**, **Galbot** (listed customers) · UniX AI / Galaxea / Spirit AI (wheeled dual-arm) · D-Robotics (RDK boards) · XREAL (AR therapy) · OriginFlow (EMG) |\n\n## Why\n- **Privacy is a real selling point in care.** Health data is sensitive; \"all inference stays on the device\" is easy for judges and clinics to understand.\n- **Offline matters in Leukerbad.** Alpine valley, thick clinic walls, guest Wi-Fi: an assistant that works with no cloud is locally relevant.\n- **Multilingual local models.** The site lists Apertus (Swiss open LLM), Mistral and OpenEuroLLM — a fit for German / French / Italian speaking guests ⚠️ on-device speed unverified.\n- **Robot VLAs on the list.** OpenVLA and pi0.5 (LeRobot) point at on-board manipulation policies for fetch-and-carry tasks.\n- **Customer overlap on site.** AgiBot and Galbot are both here, so a joint build is plausible.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams that need a ready-made robot, force-controlled contact (massage, physio) or certified medical hardware — OmniCor supplies none of it.\n- Teams that want documented APIs to code against tonight — no docs, GitHub or SDK are public.\n- Projects where cloud inference is acceptable — then the OmniCor layer adds little.\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T04-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/T04-0-Track-Overview.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (this repo)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n","Omnicor-AI-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, products]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T04 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Omnicor-AI-T04-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** OmniCor AI publishes a single one-page website with headings only. There are **no specs, no docs, no SDK, no prices and no care-sector references**. Every product row below is \"ask on site\". The role in T04 is a supporting one: make language, vision and robot-policy models run **on the device**, so patient audio and video never leave the room.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### OC Agent\n\n**Role in T04:** the tool (or tool-assisted service) that fits a model to a target board. In care this means: get a local LLM, a speech model or a robot VLA running on the robot's own computer instead of in the cloud.\n\n| Track-relevant item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| What it is | \"Agentic deployment environment\" (site wording, no further detail) |\n| Input you provide | Trained model + target hardware (per the site's contact form) |\n| Supported model families (site list) | Apertus, Mistral AI, OpenEuroLLM, Qwen, DeepSeek, OpenVLA, pi0.5 (LeRobot), \"and more\" |\n| Target silicon (ecosystem logos) | Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD |\n| Porting time per model | Not public |\n| Speech (ASR / TTS) or pose models supported | Not public |\n| ROS 2 / API surface | Not public |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified — listed on the site, no release state, no price, no download.\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Expect to work **through the OmniCor engineers**, not through a self-serve SDK.\n\n### OC Node\n\n**Role in T04:** a possible plug-in compute box beside or on a care robot, a bedside device or a therapy station.\n\n| Track-relevant item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| What it is | \"Compute platform designed in-house\" (site wording) |\n| Chip, memory, performance | Not public |\n| Power draw, battery operation | Not public |\n| Interfaces (USB, Ethernet, CAN, camera, audio) | Not public |\n| Noise / fan (matters in a patient room) | Not public |\n| Medical or safety certification | None stated |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified — whether units are on site is unknown.\n- **Developer interface:** not public. Ask whether it runs standard Linux with SSH and containers.\n\n### Model optimisation\n\n**Role in T04:** shrink or speed up a model until it meets a latency target on small hardware — e.g. a spoken reply fast enough for a hard-of-hearing guest not to give up, or a VLA fast enough for smooth arm motion.\n\n| Track-relevant item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Optimisation targets named on site | Power, speed, memory limits |\n| Methods (quantisation, distillation, compilation) | Not public |\n| Published benchmarks | None |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ⚠️ unverified — a service, terms not public.\n- **Developer interface:** none public.\n\n### European delivery\n\n**Role in T04:** not a build tool. It is a **pitch argument**: a European supply chain and European open models (Apertus, Mistral, OpenEuroLLM) support a data-sovereignty story for Swiss clinics. Details (where, who manufactures) are not public. ⚠️ unverified.\n\n## What Omnicor AI already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing public.** No deployment, pilot, demo, partner or statement in elderly care, rehabilitation, healthcare or assistive robotics was found (checked 2026-11-14).\n\nAdjacent, general evidence only:\n\n| Date | Fact | Relevance to T04 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026 (website, undated) | AgiBot, Galbot and \"sudo\" shown as teams shipping with OmniCor AI; scope not public | Both robot makers are on site — plausible joint build for assistive manipulation. [Source](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| 2026 (website, undated) | First listed domain is embodied-AI robots: perception and control on-board within a mobile power budget | Matches care robots that must work without a network. [Source](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n| 2026 (website, undated) | Model list includes multilingual LLMs and two robot VLAs | Building blocks for a voice-driven assistive robot. [Source](https://www.omnicorai.io/) |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced:** nothing. No roadmap, no healthcare plans.\n- **Inference (not a company statement):** the mix of European open LLMs, robot VLAs and a Europe-built compute node suggests a sovereignty-minded edge offer. Care and clinics would be a natural buyer for that, but OmniCor has not said so.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\nOmniCor publishes no code. The shortcuts are the **third-party open models it says it deploys**, plus standard on-device tools. None of the \"other tools\" rows are confirmed as OmniCor-supported.\n\n| Building block | Use in T04 | Facts that matter for 48 h | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Apertus** (Swiss AI Initiative) — on OmniCor's list | Local multilingual dialogue, reminders, summarising a care log | **8B and 70B** sizes, **Apache-2.0**, model card states **1,811** natively supported languages (summary line: over 1,000); quantised community builds usable with llama.cpp / Ollama. Swiss German quality ⚠️ unverified | ✅ |\n| **Mistral / Qwen / DeepSeek** — on OmniCor's list | Alternative local LLMs if Apertus is too slow or weak on a task | Exact variants OmniCor supports not public | ✅ (models) |\n| **pi0.5 in LeRobot** — on OmniCor's list | Language-conditioned fetch / tidy / hand-over on an arm | Apache-2.0 per LeRobot docs (`lerobot/pi05_base` weights card tagged `gemma`); `pip install 'lerobot[pi]'`; base checkpoint `lerobot/pi05_base`; reference fine-tune is sized for **one 80 GB GPU** — too heavy to retrain casually in 48 h | ✅ |\n| **OpenVLA** — on OmniCor's list | Same role, older VLA (`openvla-7b`, about 7.5B parameters) | Code MIT (weights inherit Llama-2 terms); trained on **970K** Open X-Embodiment trajectories; LoRA fine-tune needs roughly **27 GB+** GPU memory | ✅ |\n| whisper.cpp (other tool) | Offline speech-to-text for voice commands | MIT, runs on CPU, CUDA, ARM; fully offline | ✅ |\n| Piper (other tool) | Offline text-to-speech | GPL-3.0, local; check the voice list for German / French voices ⚠️ unverified | ✅ |\n| MediaPipe Pose Landmarker (other tool) | Rehab exercise tracking, posture and fall cues | **33 3D landmarks** per person, world coordinates in metres, built for on-device real-time use | ✅ |\n\n**Datasets:** none from OmniCor. For manipulation you will record your own demonstrations on the robot you borrow.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No body.** No robot, arm, gripper, sensors or force control. Every physical capability comes from another vendor.\n- **No care evidence.** No clinical pilot, no safety or medical-device certification, no accessibility work.\n- **No self-serve path.** No docs, SDK, GitHub or examples to prepare with; progress depends on the engineers' time, which every team will compete for.\n- **Unknown performance.** No latency, tokens-per-second or control-rate figures for any model on any board.\n- **Unknown hardware on site.** OC Node may not be here at all.\n- **Privacy claim is yours to prove.** \"On-device\" is implied by the site, not stated as a data-handling guarantee. Demonstrate it (network unplugged) rather than cite it.\n- **Research limit:** web search quota ran out during this track-specific pass; findings rest on the official site and the company profile. A care reference published elsewhere could have been missed — ask the team.\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (this repo)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n- [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [LeRobot docs — pi0.5 policy](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05)\n- [pi0.5 base model card in LeRobot (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/lerobot/pi05_base)\n- [OpenVLA GitHub repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [whisper.cpp GitHub repository](https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp)\n- [Piper TTS GitHub repository](https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl)\n- [MediaPipe Pose Landmarker guide](https://developers.google.com/edge/mediapipe/solutions/vision/pose_landmarker)\n","Omnicor-AI-T04-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Omnicor AI\"\nslug: Omnicor-AI\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, playbook]\n---\n# Omnicor AI × T04 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Omnicor AI profile](../Omnicor-AI.md) · [Brief](Omnicor-AI-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Omnicor-AI-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Ground rules for building with OmniCor AI**\n- It is the **inference layer**, not the robot. Every concept needs hardware from another vendor (or at least a laptop / edge board).\n- Nothing about OC Agent or OC Node is documented. **Build every concept so it also runs on a plain open-source edge stack** (llama.cpp / Ollama, whisper.cpp, MediaPipe); treat OmniCor as the accelerator and the on-robot port, not as a dependency.\n- Hour 0 task for all concepts: ask the OmniCor engineers what is physically here and how fast they can port a model (questions below).\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — \"Unplugged\" care companion (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Elderly guests and rehab patients need reminders (medication, therapy slots, drinking), orientation and conversation in their own language. Cloud voice assistants send audio off-site. A fully local assistant keeps health talk private and works with no network.\n- **Hardware:** OC Node if available, otherwise a Jetson-class board or D-Robotics RDK, plus microphone and speaker. Optional body: any robot with a head or screen (UniX AI Wanda, AGIBOT X2, Unitree G1) for presence only.\n- **Architecture / stack:** microphone → offline speech-to-text (whisper.cpp) → local LLM (Apertus 8B or a smaller Mistral / Qwen variant, quantised; port and tuning via OC Agent ⚠️ unverified) with a small tool set (schedule lookup, reminder, \"call nurse\" event) → offline text-to-speech (Piper). All compute on the edge box. Care schedule in a local JSON / SQLite file.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm hardware with OmniCor; get speech-in → LLM → speech-out running on a laptop in German, French and English.\n  - H6–24: move to the edge box; OmniCor optimises the LLM; add reminders and escalation tool calls; measure reply latency.\n  - H24–40: test with noisy audio and slow speech; large-text screen; optional robot body gestures.\n  - H40–48: latency numbers on a slide, rehearse, record backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Pull the network cable on stage. (2) A \"guest\" asks in German when physiotherapy is; assistant answers. (3) Switch to French: medication reminder fires, guest confirms. (4) Guest says they feel dizzy → local alert event to a \"nurse\" tablet over LAN. (5) Show latency before and after optimisation.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** LLM too slow on the board → smaller model or shorter answers; Swiss German dialect fails → ask for Standard German and say so; OmniCor has no time → run the stock quantised model, keep the privacy story.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation low-to-medium; technical depth from measured on-device latency; impact from privacy and language coverage; Leukerbad relevance high (multilingual spa and clinic guests, Swiss open model).\n\n### Concept 2 — Private rehab exercise coach\n\n- **Problem → value:** Patients do prescribed exercises alone and incorrectly; therapists cannot watch everyone. A camera-based coach counts repetitions and corrects form — but video of patients must not leave the room.\n- **Hardware:** edge box (OC Node or equivalent) + RGB camera + screen. Optional: XREAL glasses for an AR overlay; a humanoid (Unitree G1, AGIBOT X2, Magiclab MagicBot) to demonstrate the movement.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera → MediaPipe Pose Landmarker (33 3D landmarks) → joint-angle and repetition logic (plain Python, rule-based per exercise) → local LLM turns numbers into short spoken coaching and a session summary for the therapist → TTS. Only the text summary is stored; frames are discarded.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: pick 3 exercises (e.g. sit-to-stand, shoulder raise, knee extension); pose tracking and angle maths on a laptop.\n  - H6–24: repetition counter, range-of-motion thresholds, spoken feedback; port LLM to the edge box with OmniCor.\n  - H24–40: therapist summary view; optional AR overlay or robot demonstrator; test with different body sizes, seated users, loose clothing.\n  - H40–48: polish, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) \"Therapist\" selects a programme. (2) \"Patient\" does sit-to-stand; system counts and says \"lean forward a little more\". (3) Deliberately poor repetition → correction. (4) Show the therapist summary: reps, range of motion, no video stored. (5) Unplug the network to prove it is local.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** pose jitter with occlusion → restrict to frontal view and 3 exercises; LLM adds little → template sentences, LLM only for the summary; no robot demonstrator → on-screen avatar.\n- **Judging angles:** strong real-world impact (Leukerbad rehab clinics); technical depth moderate; innovation is the privacy-by-design framing. Be explicit that it is **not a medical device** and gives no clinical assessment.\n\n### Concept 3 — Night-round guardian with on-robot fall detection\n\n- **Problem → value:** Night staff are scarce; falls at night are found late. A robot patrols corridors or checks rooms on request and raises an alert — without streaming video of sleeping residents anywhere.\n- **Hardware:** a mobile base from another vendor (quadruped: Unitree Go2, AGIBOT D1, Genisom, Magiclab MagicDog; or a wheeled humanoid) + its camera + OC Node or the robot's own computer running the OmniCor-optimised models.\n- **Architecture / stack:** robot camera → on-board person detection and pose (lying on floor, no movement for N seconds) → local LLM / VLM composes a text alert (\"person on floor, corridor 2, responsive: unknown\") → LAN message to a nurse tablet; robot asks \"are you okay?\" via local speech stack and reports the answer. Navigation uses the robot vendor's own stack or waypoint teleoperation — do not write a navigation stack in 48 h.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: secure a robot; confirm camera access and where extra compute can be mounted and powered.\n  - H6–24: fall heuristic on laptop with recorded clips; port to on-board compute with OmniCor; alert channel.\n  - H24–40: integrate with patrol route; voice check-in; false-alarm tests (sitting, kneeling, bending).\n  - H40–48: rehearse on the demo floor, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Robot walks a short route. (2) Team member lies down; robot stops, asks if they are okay. (3) No answer → alert appears on tablet as text only. (4) Second pass: person kneels to tie a shoe → no alarm. (5) Show that no image left the robot.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** robot access or camera API blocked → fixed camera on a tripod plus the same pipeline; false alarms → require stillness plus failed voice check; compute mounting issues → tethered edge box on LAN.\n- **Judging angles:** clear caregiver relief; technical depth in on-board perception under a power budget (OmniCor's stated core case); Leukerbad relevance via clinics and hotels with elderly guests.\n\n### Concept 4 — Voice-commanded fetch-and-hand-over with an on-board VLA (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** People with limited mobility need small things fetched (water bottle, glasses, remote). A robot that understands a spoken request and manipulates objects **with all models on-board** is the full assistive-robot story.\n- **Hardware:** wheeled dual-arm mobile manipulator from an OmniCor-listed customer — **AGIBOT G1 / G2** or **Galbot G1** — or Galaxea R1, Spirit AI Moz1, UniX AI Wanda. Compute: robot's own computer and/or OC Node.\n- **Architecture / stack:** speech → local LLM parses the request into a short task string → VLA policy (pi0.5 via LeRobot, OpenVLA, or the robot vendor's own model such as GO-1, GraspVLA or G0) produces arm actions → vendor low-level controller. OmniCor's job: make the VLA and the LLM share the on-board compute at a usable control rate ⚠️ unverified whether a port already exists for the robot you get.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: **go / no-go** — is there a robot already running a VLA through OmniCor? If not, drop to the fallback now.\n  - H6–24: one object, one table, one hand-over location; record demonstrations if fine-tuning is feasible (pi0.5 reference fine-tune assumes an 80 GB GPU — check what GPUs exist on site); voice front-end in parallel.\n  - H24–40: integrate voice → task → policy; add second object only if the first is reliable; safe hand-over pose, low speed.\n  - H40–48: 10 consecutive rehearsals, record the best run.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Seated \"resident\" asks in German for the water bottle. (2) Robot confirms verbally, picks the bottle, brings it to a hand-over tray. (3) Second request in French for glasses. (4) Network unplugged throughout; show on-board resource use.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** fine-tuning not feasible → use the vendor's pretrained grasping model or scripted grasps with the local LLM as planner; control rate too low → run the VLA on a tethered edge box; safety near people → hand over via tray, never directly to a hand, keep an operator on the stop button.\n- **Judging angles:** highest innovation and technical depth if it works; highest failure risk. Leukerbad relevance: clinic and hotel rooms, weak in-room connectivity.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| OmniCor AI + AGIBOT (G1 / G2) or Galbot (G1) | On-board VLA fetch-and-carry; both are listed OmniCor customers, so a port may already exist ⚠️ unverified | Medium if a port exists, high otherwise |\n| OmniCor AI + D-Robotics RDK | Low-cost bedside companion or coach box; tests OmniCor's \"any device\" claim on small silicon ⚠️ support unverified | Low to medium |\n| OmniCor AI + XREAL | AR-guided therapy with pose and coaching computed locally | Medium (AR app work) |\n| OmniCor AI + Unitree Go2 / AGIBOT D1 / Genisom | Night-round patrol with on-robot fall detection | Medium (camera access, mounting, power) |\n| OmniCor AI + UniX AI Wanda / Spirit AI Moz1 / Galaxea R1 | Service humanoid with private voice interface | Medium to high (no known prior port) |\n| OmniCor AI + Flexiv / ROKAE | Local voice control for a force-controlled physio arm; OmniCor adds only the language layer | Medium; contact safety is the hard part and not OmniCor's |\n| OmniCor AI + OriginFlow | EMG intent signals classified on-device for rehab feedback | High (new signal type, no known support) |\n| OmniCor AI + Binabik AI / coprod | Swiss software and hardware stack with on-device inference underneath | Unknown — ask both teams |\n\n## Questions to ask the Omnicor AI engineers on site\n\n1. What did you bring: OC Node units (how many, which chip, memory, power, ports), or only software and people?\n2. Which robots here already run a model deployed by you — AGIBOT, Galbot? Which model, at what control rate?\n3. Which LLM can you run fully offline for a spoken dialogue, and what reply latency and tokens per second should we expect on your hardware?\n4. Can you port a model we choose (e.g. Apertus 8B, a pi0.5 fine-tune) within the first 6–12 hours? What do you need from us?\n5. How do we call the model: ROS 2 node, HTTP / gRPC endpoint, Python library? Is there any sample code?\n6. Do you support speech models (ASR / TTS) and small vision models (pose, detection), or only LLMs and VLAs?\n7. Can an LLM and a VLA run at the same time on one OC Node, and what is the power draw on battery?\n8. Can you show, technically, that no data leaves the device (no telemetry, no licence call-home)? We want to claim that on stage.\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Omnicor AI for T04 if** your idea's core value is privacy or offline operation, you already have a robot or device from another vendor, and the OmniCor engineers confirm in the first hours that they can port your model in time.\n- **Avoid if** you need a physical robot, force-controlled contact or documented APIs from this vendor, or if your concept works just as well with cloud inference.\n\n## Sources\n- [OmniCor AI official website](https://www.omnicorai.io/)\n- [Omnicor AI company profile (this repo)](../Omnicor-AI.md)\n- [Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509 model card (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/swiss-ai/Apertus-8B-Instruct-2509)\n- [LeRobot docs — pi0.5 policy](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/pi05)\n- [OpenVLA GitHub repository](https://github.com/openvla/openvla)\n- [whisper.cpp GitHub repository](https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp)\n- [Piper TTS GitHub repository](https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl)\n- [MediaPipe Pose Landmarker guide](https://developers.google.com/edge/mediapipe/solutions/vision/pose_landmarker)\n"}});
